WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.936 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.936 --> 00:00:03.388 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.388 --> 00:00:04.840 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:12.590 align:middle line:90%


00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:15.470 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Go ahead and turn
to your neighbor and start.

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:17.630 align:middle line:84%
One thing you could do is
pull out the main reading

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:19.713 align:middle line:84%
that we did about situated
learning, because we'll

00:00:19.713 --> 00:00:21.180 align:middle line:90%
do a close read of that today.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.310 align:middle line:90%
So pull that up on something.

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:26.160 align:middle line:84%
And then, in the minutes that we
have-- we usually start at 1:05.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.080 align:middle line:84%
But in the minutes that we have,
turn to your neighbor and start

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:34.200 align:middle line:84%
thinking about why babies
would give cognitive load

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.888 align:middle line:90%
theorists conniptions.

00:00:36.888 --> 00:00:39.714 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:00:39.714 --> 00:00:40.520 align:middle line:90%


00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:43.593 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think the
first one is like--

00:00:43.593 --> 00:00:45.260 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Right, like
when you're a kid.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:45.920 align:middle line:90%
It's like--

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:48.830 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Just as one
level-setting thing, in case

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:51.170 align:middle line:84%
you haven't hung out
with babies in a while,

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:53.010 align:middle line:84%
in their first
few years of life,

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:55.520 align:middle line:84%
how would you characterize
the amount and rate

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:57.542 align:middle line:90%
of learning that babies do?

00:00:57.542 --> 00:00:58.250 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Insane.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:00:59.430 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Insane.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:02.210 align:middle line:84%
It is bonkers how
much babies learn.

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:04.239 align:middle line:84%
I mean, a very
strong feeling I had

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:07.600 align:middle line:84%
when I was first
a father was like,

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.780 align:middle line:84%
I do not improve
at all in a week

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:13.280 align:middle line:84%
or in a month or
really most years.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:15.040 align:middle line:84%
I'm really not that
much better than I was

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:15.980 align:middle line:90%
at the beginning of the year.

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:17.563 align:middle line:84%
And you're just
watching these infants

00:01:17.563 --> 00:01:21.430 align:middle line:84%
who are totally transformed
because they are learning things

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:26.650 align:middle line:84%
at an amazing rate that you
can see daily, weekly, monthly.

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:29.350 align:middle line:90%
Incredibly accelerated learning.

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:32.440 align:middle line:84%
So we'll just use
that to level set.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:36.280 align:middle line:84%
Let's take it as a given that
babies learn an amazing amount

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.470 align:middle line:90%
in their early years.

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:39.530 align:middle line:90%
Now, keep talking.

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:41.240 align:middle line:84%
Why would that
prove to be such--

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.090 align:middle line:84%
why would that give
cognitive load theorists

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:44.860 align:middle line:90%
such a conniption?

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:47.920 align:middle line:84%
And you can think about how it
connects to some of the stuff

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:50.087 align:middle line:84%
that you've been reading
about situated load theory.

00:01:50.087 --> 00:01:51.040 align:middle line:90%
Keep talking.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.670 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I guess presumably--

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:54.920 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: All right, good.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:58.070 align:middle line:84%
What are some observations
that people made?

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:00.140 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: All my good
habits of schooling.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:03.050 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: What are--

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:04.820 align:middle line:90%
babies are significant.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:07.875 align:middle line:84%
So what are some of the
observations that you made?

00:02:07.875 --> 00:02:10.419 align:middle line:90%


00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:13.150 align:middle line:84%
What were you guys
talking about in the back?

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:15.340 align:middle line:84%
What was your group
talking about, Gloria?

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:18.200 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was saying that if
cognitive load theory was true,

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.985 align:middle line:84%
then that would say that babies
probably can't learn anything

00:02:20.985 --> 00:02:23.110 align:middle line:84%
because everything is
extraneous knowledge to them,

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:25.250 align:middle line:84%
and they don't have
any long-term memory.

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:26.930 align:middle line:84%
They haven't formed
any feelings yet.

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:27.260 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: All right.

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:27.760 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:28.952 align:middle line:90%
So they're not drawing in.

00:02:28.952 --> 00:02:31.160 align:middle line:84%
They don't have a lot of
long-term memory to draw on.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:34.390 align:middle line:84%
They are the
canonical us looking

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:37.000 align:middle line:84%
at Sanskrit letters,
where everything

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.118 align:middle line:90%
is its own discrete part.

00:02:39.118 --> 00:02:40.910 align:middle line:84%
So you would think they
are working memory.

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:43.390 align:middle line:84%
I mean, this video, I
think, is helpful for that.

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:46.520 align:middle line:84%
Everything on this screen
is brand new to them.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:48.440 align:middle line:84%
There's nothing that
they've ever seen before.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:49.270 align:middle line:90%
And it's filled.

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:51.430 align:middle line:84%
There's a slide and a
dad and that other thing

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:53.300 align:middle line:84%
back there and all this
stuff on the ground,

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.330 align:middle line:84%
and people are talking,
and the sun is out,

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:57.310 align:middle line:84%
and all these things
are happening at once.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:57.810 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:00.870 align:middle line:90%
What else do people talk about?

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:04.890 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's also like, the
speed with which they learn

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:07.620 align:middle line:84%
doesn't really connect
with the bottleneck theory

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:12.210 align:middle line:84%
and the theory of-- it
takes a while for you

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:14.680 align:middle line:84%
to compile all of
this information,

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:16.680 align:middle line:84%
but it almost seems like
a baby sees it one time

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:18.100 align:middle line:90%
and is able to retain it.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:21.030 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, babies
seem to do exactly the thing

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:24.670 align:middle line:84%
that Sweller said people
should not be able to do,

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:25.950 align:middle line:90%
which is to be--

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:26.820 align:middle line:90%
I mean, would you--

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:28.945 align:middle line:84%
like, this baby learning
stuff, would you generally

00:03:28.945 --> 00:03:31.770 align:middle line:84%
characterize their experience
as open-ended problem

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:34.223 align:middle line:84%
solving, or being
carefully walked

00:03:34.223 --> 00:03:35.723 align:middle line:84%
through a series
of worked examples?

00:03:35.723 --> 00:03:39.120 align:middle line:90%


00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.570 align:middle line:90%
Well, how would you describe it?

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:41.868 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:03:41.868 --> 00:03:44.160 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, I mean,
I'm just going on the slide

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:45.330 align:middle line:90%
and going down.

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:46.920 align:middle line:90%
It's not like dad's like--

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:50.530 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I think you'd find more
structure and stuff like that,

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.750 align:middle line:84%
but there's a lot of just
like, wow, I'm a baby.

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:53.410 align:middle line:90%
I'm out there.

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:55.460 align:middle line:90%
I'm doing my thing.

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:58.820 align:middle line:84%
And the worked example effect
and the expert reversal effect

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:00.930 align:middle line:84%
suggests that should
not be very effective.

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:03.590 align:middle line:84%
The most effective
way to teach babies

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:07.460 align:middle line:84%
should be through careful,
devolving scaffolding

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:08.940 align:middle line:84%
of language and
things like that.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:10.023 align:middle line:90%
And that's not what we do.

00:04:10.023 --> 00:04:12.800 align:middle line:84%
We just throw babies in
with people all the time.

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.270 align:middle line:90%
And they learn language.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:16.110 align:middle line:90%
They learn movement.

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:19.700 align:middle line:84%
They learn a few other things
at an extraordinary rate.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:23.960 align:middle line:84%
So what connection do
you see between this set

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:25.525 align:middle line:90%
of ideas and situated learning?

00:04:25.525 --> 00:04:29.000 align:middle line:90%


00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.280 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: There's a lot
of trial and error in it.

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:32.870 align:middle line:84%
We were talking
about, hey, when they

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:34.430 align:middle line:84%
see a block they
want to go get, they

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:35.600 align:middle line:84%
start to figure out
the different ways

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:37.642 align:middle line:84%
that they can go get that
block but through trial

00:04:37.642 --> 00:04:39.642 align:middle line:84%
and error-- trying to
stand up, trying to crawl,

00:04:39.642 --> 00:04:40.890 align:middle line:90%
trying to drag themselves.

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:41.090 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:42.320 align:middle line:90%
All that kind of stuff.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:43.740 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE], come on in.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:44.275 align:middle line:90%
Sit with me.

00:04:44.275 --> 00:04:44.550 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Sorry.

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:45.620 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: No, no.

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:47.240 align:middle line:90%
Delighted to have you.

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:48.800 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:52.490 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, I mean, this certainly
looks a lot more like situated

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:56.870 align:middle line:84%
learning than it does look like
a carefully sculpted classroom.

00:04:56.870 --> 00:05:02.077 align:middle line:84%
And so for situated
learning people--

00:05:02.077 --> 00:05:04.410 align:middle line:84%
I think we'll see this more
in some stuff that we read--

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:07.610 align:middle line:84%
the experience of babies
seems like something that's

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:10.040 align:middle line:90%
pretty important to draw from.

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:16.160 align:middle line:84%
How would you characterize
the feelings that babies have

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:17.940 align:middle line:90%
as they learn language?

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:19.530 align:middle line:90%
Are they happy?

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:20.310 align:middle line:90%
Are they sad?

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:21.060 align:middle line:90%
Are they angry?

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:21.930 align:middle line:90%
Are they frustrated?

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:22.770 align:middle line:90%
Are they joyful?

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:23.630 align:middle line:90%
Are they playful?

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:25.130 align:middle line:84%
What kind of feelings
do babies seem

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:28.377 align:middle line:84%
to have as they're
learning words?

00:05:28.377 --> 00:05:29.460 align:middle line:90%
What would you say, Jaren?

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.793 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was going to say,
they seem kind of fearless

00:05:31.793 --> 00:05:34.850 align:middle line:84%
and like there's no social
pressure to try and pronounce

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:35.790 align:middle line:90%
this word correctly.

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:37.230 align:middle line:90%
They'll just say the word.

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:38.660 align:middle line:90%
And we'll be like, oh, good job.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:41.490 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Just make noises
come out of our mouths whenever.

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:43.500 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, do you want to
grab another chair in?

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:45.320 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It doesn't
seem like there's

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:49.850 align:middle line:84%
a lot of fear behind trying to
practice what they're learning.

00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:51.960 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But I think,
in addition to that,

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:54.112 align:middle line:84%
there can be a lot of
frustration as well.

00:05:54.112 --> 00:05:54.820 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: OK.

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:55.030 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:55.530 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:57.425 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The consequences
of not knowing

00:05:57.425 --> 00:05:58.550 align:middle line:90%
how to say specific things.

00:05:58.550 --> 00:05:58.720 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:00.880 align:middle line:84%
Or if you watch
babies when they're--

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:04.060 align:middle line:84%
I mean, they cry when they can't
articulate the things that they

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:05.410 align:middle line:90%
want to be able to say.

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:05.930 align:middle line:90%
What are you thinking?

00:06:05.930 --> 00:06:07.722 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, in
addition to frustration,

00:06:07.722 --> 00:06:10.080 align:middle line:84%
I think there's a bit of
happiness when they actually

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:11.830 align:middle line:84%
achieve what they're
trying to communicate

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:12.898 align:middle line:90%
and they feel understood.

00:06:12.898 --> 00:06:13.690 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:16.790 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think there's
some sort of pride in that.

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:19.065 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: What were
you going to say, Courtney?

00:06:19.065 --> 00:06:19.690 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Same.

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:20.920 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Same
kind of thing.

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:22.060 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I also wonder--

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:24.310 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: My
daughter, we used to--

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:25.670 align:middle line:90%
we used to just--

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:27.050 align:middle line:90%
I guess it was my younger one.

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:29.195 align:middle line:84%
We used to just list
vegetables and list things

00:06:29.195 --> 00:06:30.320 align:middle line:90%
you get at the supermarket.

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:32.450 align:middle line:84%
And she thought that was
the funniest thing ever.

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:35.262 align:middle line:84%
You would just be like,
pears, [SCREECHES]

00:06:35.262 --> 00:06:37.220 align:middle line:84%
Just in the back of the
car, just saying stuff.

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:39.085 align:middle line:84%
And she's giggling
the whole time.

00:06:39.085 --> 00:06:40.900 align:middle line:84%
I mean, if you watch
a video like this,

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:44.500 align:middle line:90%
this is clearly delightful.

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:46.810 align:middle line:84%
There's not a lot that's
Sweller and colleagues have

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:49.720 align:middle line:90%
to say about being delighted.

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:57.000 align:middle line:84%
I think, if you were to
situate the moral ethic of work

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:59.920 align:middle line:84%
around learning in
cognitive load theory,

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:02.080 align:middle line:84%
it's very, like,
learning is hard.

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:04.860 align:middle line:84%
We persevere through
a hard thing,

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:06.900 align:middle line:84%
and we control
the hardness of it

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:09.150 align:middle line:84%
so that we can
scaffold it for people.

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:12.420 align:middle line:84%
And for situated learning
people, they aren't really--

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:13.558 align:middle line:90%
these aren't arguments.

00:07:13.558 --> 00:07:15.100 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes they're
explicit arguments.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:17.600 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes they're just tones
that you find in the way people

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:18.790 align:middle line:90%
talk about these things.

00:07:18.790 --> 00:07:20.435 align:middle line:84%
They'd be like, oh,
learning is great.

00:07:20.435 --> 00:07:22.810 align:middle line:84%
Learning is the thing that we
do and get better at stuff.

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:25.273 align:middle line:84%
It's nice and natural
and things like that.

00:07:25.273 --> 00:07:26.940 align:middle line:84%
Can anybody think of
a thing that you've

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:29.320 align:middle line:84%
learned in which you
got better at the thing,

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:33.420 align:middle line:84%
and it was just playful,
enjoyable, natural to get better

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:34.710 align:middle line:90%
at it?

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:36.450 align:middle line:84%
Anybody can shout
out one or two things

00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:39.060 align:middle line:90%
that was like that for you.

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:41.590 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: When I first learned
how to use a power drill.

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:42.150 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Awesome.

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:42.390 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:43.723 align:middle line:90%
Learning how to use power drill.

00:07:43.723 --> 00:07:46.750 align:middle line:84%
Very satisfying, immediate
feedback, things like that.

00:07:46.750 --> 00:07:47.460 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:49.800 align:middle line:84%
Other things that people
really enjoyed learning?

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:51.980 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think,
similarly, tactical things,

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:53.390 align:middle line:90%
like learning how to do pottery.

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:54.390 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Oh, great.

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:56.698 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, pottery seems
like a good one.

00:07:56.698 --> 00:07:57.990 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Learning how to swim.

00:07:57.990 --> 00:07:58.970 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH:
Learning how to swim.

00:07:58.970 --> 00:07:59.250 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:00.360 align:middle line:84%
We're saying a lot
of physical things.

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:00.860 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:02.000 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Video games.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:03.360 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Video games.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:07.722 align:middle line:84%
Video games are-- there
is a whole science.

00:08:07.722 --> 00:08:08.430 align:middle line:90%
We won't read it.

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:11.068 align:middle line:84%
There's a guy named James
Gee, who wrote a great book

00:08:11.068 --> 00:08:12.360 align:middle line:90%
about learning and video games.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:13.777 align:middle line:84%
It's a little bit
like Tocqueville

00:08:13.777 --> 00:08:16.520 align:middle line:84%
coming to America, if that's
a reference that you know.

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:19.730 align:middle line:84%
But Tocqueville was this
European who came to America,

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:23.300 align:middle line:84%
and he saw America incredibly
intimately in a way

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:27.180 align:middle line:84%
that only an outsider could,
in part by becoming an insider.

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:29.000 align:middle line:84%
James Gee is an old
guy who was like, why

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:30.600 align:middle line:84%
do my kids like
video games so much?

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:31.933 align:middle line:90%
And he starts playing with them.

00:08:31.933 --> 00:08:35.220 align:middle line:84%
He's like, these are amazing
learning experiences.

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:37.220 align:middle line:84%
Think about someone
learning a really hard game,

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:40.140 align:middle line:84%
like Civilization
6 or Stellaris,

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:42.559 align:middle line:84%
or put in whatever game
you've been playing recently

00:08:42.559 --> 00:08:45.327 align:middle line:84%
that just has incredibly
complicated systems in them.

00:08:45.327 --> 00:08:46.910 align:middle line:84%
There aren't a lot
of people who are--

00:08:46.910 --> 00:08:51.150 align:middle line:84%
Minecraft, redstone building
in Minecraft, stuff like that.

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:52.740 align:middle line:84%
There aren't people
who are like, god,

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:54.282 align:middle line:84%
it was such a drag
that I had to take

00:08:54.282 --> 00:08:57.180 align:middle line:84%
all those classes on redstone
to be able to, blah, blah, blah.

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:00.660 align:middle line:84%
These games have systems
built within them.

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:03.680 align:middle line:84%
They're-- almost all of them--
canonically situated kinds

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:06.600 align:middle line:84%
of things, where you're just
immersed in the environment,

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:07.715 align:middle line:90%
you're encouraged to play.

00:09:07.715 --> 00:09:09.590 align:middle line:84%
There are some peer
structures around to help

00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:10.840 align:middle line:90%
you learn and stuff like that.

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:14.840 align:middle line:84%
But it's extremely
pleasant, even when

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:17.250 align:middle line:84%
people might find it
frustrating or things like that.

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:19.160 align:middle line:84%
I think a lot of--
in families, cooking

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:21.060 align:middle line:84%
is something that--
learning how to cook,

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:24.030 align:middle line:84%
which has both tactile and
scientific measurement,

00:09:24.030 --> 00:09:26.120 align:middle line:84%
mathematical,
language components

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:28.555 align:middle line:84%
that people often find
quite pleasurable.

00:09:28.555 --> 00:09:29.930 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And this
connects a lot

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:33.088 align:middle line:84%
to the material I'm learning
in CMS 587 about assessments.

00:09:33.088 --> 00:09:34.880 align:middle line:84%
We're learning about
the different theories

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:37.070 align:middle line:84%
of intelligence and
how, if you believe

00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:39.920 align:middle line:84%
that your intelligence isn't
fixed and it can expand,

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:41.220 align:middle line:90%
then you can learn a lot more.

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:43.262 align:middle line:84%
And that's kind of like
young children or babies,

00:09:43.262 --> 00:09:45.450 align:middle line:84%
because they're not
really afraid of failure.

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:47.410 align:middle line:84%
And they're just--
they're very resilient.

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:48.140 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:49.280 align:middle line:90%
They don't care what's going on.

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:51.100 align:middle line:84%
They're just trying
to figure things out.

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:53.828 align:middle line:84%
How many of you
have had experiences

00:09:53.828 --> 00:09:56.120 align:middle line:84%
that you're really happy
with, where learning was hard,

00:09:56.120 --> 00:09:58.510 align:middle line:84%
where you persevered
through a challenging

00:09:58.510 --> 00:10:00.957 align:middle line:84%
set of learning experiences
and got better at it?

00:10:00.957 --> 00:10:02.540 align:middle line:84%
And you were like,
yeah, that's great.

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:03.920 align:middle line:90%
I'm super glad I did that.

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:06.200 align:middle line:84%
What things come to
mind for that for you?

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:08.678 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think, for me,
learning to code was like that.

00:10:08.678 --> 00:10:10.720 align:middle line:84%
I think differently from
some people in the room.

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:12.387 align:middle line:84%
I didn't learn to
code in middle school.

00:10:12.387 --> 00:10:13.730 align:middle line:90%
I learned it in university.

00:10:13.730 --> 00:10:16.780 align:middle line:84%
So there were traits associated
with it and things like that.

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:16.880 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:17.090 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:10:17.090 --> 00:10:19.750 align:middle line:84%
You watched a bunch of lectures
and did Psets and turned them

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:20.250 align:middle line:90%
in.

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:22.510 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But I remember
the first very complex Pset

00:10:22.510 --> 00:10:24.910 align:middle line:84%
that I completed and my
program worked perfectly.

00:10:24.910 --> 00:10:26.530 align:middle line:90%
And I was like--

00:10:26.530 --> 00:10:27.760 align:middle line:90%
it was an amazing feeling.

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:28.720 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: It
felt very satisfying,

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:30.610 align:middle line:84%
even though some of the learning
experience of doing that-- yeah,

00:10:30.610 --> 00:10:31.110 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:32.485 align:middle line:90%
So that kind of--

00:10:32.485 --> 00:10:34.510 align:middle line:84%
I think there's a
moral story that

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:37.270 align:middle line:84%
emerges around a lot of
instructionist learning, which

00:10:37.270 --> 00:10:38.810 align:middle line:90%
is like, it was hard.

00:10:38.810 --> 00:10:40.400 align:middle line:84%
We put our nose
to the grindstone.

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:41.390 align:middle line:90%
We sacrificed.

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:44.030 align:middle line:84%
But after that sacrifice, we had
this great feeling of relief.

00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:45.490 align:middle line:84%
And I think most of us
have some kind of learning

00:10:45.490 --> 00:10:46.805 align:middle line:90%
experience that goes like that.

00:10:46.805 --> 00:10:48.180 align:middle line:84%
And then situated
learning people

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:52.140 align:middle line:84%
have more of the story of, we
just naturally found ourselves

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:55.120 align:middle line:84%
in this space surrounded
by opportunities to learn.

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:58.450 align:middle line:84%
And it was playful, and
it felt easy and natural.

00:10:58.450 --> 00:11:00.512 align:middle line:84%
And that's what we're
trying to restore there.

00:11:00.512 --> 00:11:02.220 align:middle line:84%
So actually, the
cognitive load theorists

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:05.010 align:middle line:84%
have, to me, what I think is a
funny way of dealing with this.

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:06.870 align:middle line:84%
There's just a guy who
proposed that there's

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:09.420 align:middle line:84%
two categories of
things that are learned,

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:12.420 align:middle line:84%
called biologically primary
and biologically secondary

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:13.800 align:middle line:90%
kinds of things.

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:18.240 align:middle line:84%
And we're evolutionarily
wired to naturally learn

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:20.350 align:middle line:84%
biologically primary
kinds of things,

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:22.287 align:middle line:84%
and everything else is
biologically secondary

00:11:22.287 --> 00:11:24.120 align:middle line:84%
and is much harder, so
that you're naturally

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:26.220 align:middle line:84%
wired to learn how
to walk, how to eat

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:29.590 align:middle line:84%
some basic parts of
socializing, recognizing faces.

00:11:29.590 --> 00:11:32.580 align:middle line:84%
Language ends up being a real
problem for this because it's

00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:34.500 align:middle line:84%
like, are you
biologically primed

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:38.080 align:middle line:84%
to learn your first language,
to learn multiple languages?

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:39.940 align:middle line:90%
Why would you be biologically--

00:11:39.940 --> 00:11:42.790 align:middle line:84%
why was it easy for me to
learn English when I was young,

00:11:42.790 --> 00:11:45.690 align:middle line:84%
but Spanish was really
hard when I was older?

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:46.920 align:middle line:90%
Did my brain change?

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:49.470 align:middle line:90%
Did evolution-- anyway.

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:52.530 align:middle line:84%
I actually find this theory
totally unsatisfying.

00:11:52.530 --> 00:11:55.620 align:middle line:84%
Are any of you familiar
with the idea of epicycles?

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:58.710 align:middle line:84%
Before the Copernican
revolution,

00:11:58.710 --> 00:12:01.397 align:middle line:84%
everybody thought orbits
were circles, not ovals

00:12:01.397 --> 00:12:03.730 align:middle line:84%
or whatever it is that they're
supposed to-- what is it?

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:04.650 align:middle line:90%
What's the shape of them?

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:05.120 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Ellipses.

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:06.078 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Ellipses.

00:12:06.078 --> 00:12:09.758 align:middle line:84%
And there was a period of
time when they realized, oh,

00:12:09.758 --> 00:12:11.050 align:middle line:90%
circle measurements don't work.

00:12:11.050 --> 00:12:13.410 align:middle line:84%
And so they added
circles to the circles.

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:17.160 align:middle line:84%
They believed that there had
to be perfect circles making

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:17.770 align:middle line:90%
the orbits.

00:12:17.770 --> 00:12:19.395 align:middle line:84%
And so they believed
that somehow there

00:12:19.395 --> 00:12:21.810 align:middle line:90%
were orbits within the orbits.

00:12:21.810 --> 00:12:23.430 align:middle line:84%
And then I think
it was Copernicus

00:12:23.430 --> 00:12:25.390 align:middle line:84%
who came around and was like,
actually, it's just like this.

00:12:25.390 --> 00:12:26.557 align:middle line:90%
And that works a lot better.

00:12:26.557 --> 00:12:29.370 align:middle line:84%
But to me, biologically primary
and biologically secondary

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:32.140 align:middle line:84%
things operate-- that seems
a little bit like an epicycle

00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:32.640 align:middle line:90%
to me.

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:35.470 align:middle line:84%
We haven't quite figured
out what's going on there.

00:12:35.470 --> 00:12:40.200 align:middle line:84%
But that is how, canonically,
the cognitive load theory people

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:43.130 align:middle line:84%
just wave all this away and
say, ah, babies, whatever,

00:12:43.130 --> 00:12:44.190 align:middle line:90%
biologically primary.

00:12:44.190 --> 00:12:45.718 align:middle line:84%
We don't have to
worry about them.

00:12:45.718 --> 00:12:48.260 align:middle line:84%
Everything after you're four
years old or something like that

00:12:48.260 --> 00:12:50.700 align:middle line:90%
is cognitive load theory works.

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:52.400 align:middle line:84%
But for the situated
learning people,

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:54.740 align:middle line:84%
not exactly, but
in a lot of ways

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:57.140 align:middle line:84%
they're trying to recapture
the learning experiences

00:12:57.140 --> 00:12:58.970 align:middle line:84%
that we have when
we're very young,

00:12:58.970 --> 00:13:02.910 align:middle line:84%
where learning is
natural, playful.

00:13:02.910 --> 00:13:06.320 align:middle line:84%
It's not always happy, but
often socially satisfying

00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:07.825 align:middle line:90%
and extremely effective.

00:13:07.825 --> 00:13:09.950 align:middle line:84%
I mean, what babies learn
in a short period of time

00:13:09.950 --> 00:13:11.360 align:middle line:90%
is just bonkers.

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:13.220 align:middle line:84%
It's one of the most
fun things about being

00:13:13.220 --> 00:13:15.240 align:middle line:84%
a parent, or an
auntie, or an uncle,

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:18.380 align:middle line:84%
or all the other ways you get to
spend a lot of time with kids.

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:18.880 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:21.800 align:middle line:90%


00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:23.935 align:middle line:90%
Let's just get oriented here.

00:13:23.935 --> 00:13:28.010 align:middle line:90%


00:13:28.010 --> 00:13:30.380 align:middle line:90%
So again, we're in foundations.

00:13:30.380 --> 00:13:35.030 align:middle line:84%
We're trying to get a hand of
these two contemporary learning

00:13:35.030 --> 00:13:38.060 align:middle line:84%
theories, cognitive load
theory, situated learning,

00:13:38.060 --> 00:13:42.940 align:middle line:84%
that these two ideas about
how people learn are ancient,

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:44.942 align:middle line:84%
that you can find
people like Plutarch,

00:13:44.942 --> 00:13:47.150 align:middle line:84%
"For the mind does not
require filling like a bottle,

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:49.960 align:middle line:84%
but rather, like wood, it only
requires kindling to create

00:13:49.960 --> 00:13:52.300 align:middle line:84%
in it an impulse to think
independently and an ardent

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:54.362 align:middle line:90%
desire for truth."

00:13:54.362 --> 00:13:57.970 align:middle line:84%
This is Plutarch making a
case for situated learning

00:13:57.970 --> 00:13:59.450 align:middle line:84%
and against cognitive
load theory,

00:13:59.450 --> 00:14:00.910 align:middle line:84%
though he wouldn't have known
those terms because they

00:14:00.910 --> 00:14:02.890 align:middle line:84%
didn't show up for
another 2,000 years.

00:14:02.890 --> 00:14:06.200 align:middle line:84%
But you can hear the resonance
of the debate in his mind.

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:10.360 align:middle line:84%
Here's a little chunk from
a 2017 New Yorker article.

00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:13.390 align:middle line:84%
"For nearly a century, public
education in America has been

00:14:13.390 --> 00:14:15.760 align:middle line:84%
influenced by two opposing
pedagogical approaches--

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:17.390 align:middle line:84%
traditionalism and
progressivism."

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:19.890 align:middle line:84%
A thing that you've got to catch
is that there are these two

00:14:19.890 --> 00:14:22.310 align:middle line:84%
ideas that each of them
has 8,000 different names.

00:14:22.310 --> 00:14:24.133 align:middle line:84%
But anytime you
notice this dichotomy.

00:14:24.133 --> 00:14:26.050 align:middle line:84%
"Broadly speaking, in
the traditional approach

00:14:26.050 --> 00:14:28.592 align:middle line:84%
to education, a teacher imparts
knowledge to students through

00:14:28.592 --> 00:14:31.450 align:middle line:84%
direct instruction and embodies
a disciplinary culture in which

00:14:31.450 --> 00:14:33.560 align:middle line:84%
obedience is both
prized and rewarded."

00:14:33.560 --> 00:14:36.260 align:middle line:90%
That's a nice-- she frames it.

00:14:36.260 --> 00:14:37.508 align:middle line:90%
Here's the pedagogical piece.

00:14:37.508 --> 00:14:39.550 align:middle line:84%
Knowledge is imparted
through direct instruction.

00:14:39.550 --> 00:14:43.030 align:middle line:84%
"And embodies a disciplinary
culture in which obedience is

00:14:43.030 --> 00:14:44.170 align:middle line:90%
prized and rewarded."

00:14:44.170 --> 00:14:45.932 align:middle line:84%
Obedience was not
prized and rewarded

00:14:45.932 --> 00:14:47.390 align:middle line:84%
amongst the baby
on the playground.

00:14:47.390 --> 00:14:49.807 align:middle line:84%
What was rewarded
was screwing around.

00:14:49.807 --> 00:14:52.390 align:middle line:84%
"The purpose of the classroom
is to equip all students to meet

00:14:52.390 --> 00:14:53.640 align:middle line:90%
measurable academic standards.

00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:56.200 align:middle line:84%
At progressive institutions, a
teacher develops a curriculum

00:14:56.200 --> 00:14:58.120 align:middle line:84%
but urges students to
treat it as a staging

00:14:58.120 --> 00:15:00.102 align:middle line:84%
ground for their own
intellectual discoveries,

00:15:00.102 --> 00:15:02.060 align:middle line:84%
often through hands-on
activity and group work.

00:15:02.060 --> 00:15:03.977 align:middle line:84%
Allowances are made for
differences in the way

00:15:03.977 --> 00:15:05.210 align:middle line:90%
individual students learn.

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:07.270 align:middle line:84%
Progressivism was
inspired, in large part,

00:15:07.270 --> 00:15:09.490 align:middle line:84%
by the work of John Dewey,
the American philosopher

00:15:09.490 --> 00:15:11.010 align:middle line:90%
and education theorist."

00:15:11.010 --> 00:15:12.260 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I'll say that last line.

00:15:12.260 --> 00:15:14.843 align:middle line:84%
"For Dewey, the classroom was
not simply a place for acquiring

00:15:14.843 --> 00:15:17.800 align:middle line:84%
academic credentials; it was
also a venue in which students

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:21.560 align:middle line:84%
learned crucial values about
being citizens in a democracy."

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:26.510 align:middle line:84%
So maybe these things are laden
not just with pedagogical ideas,

00:15:26.510 --> 00:15:31.850 align:middle line:84%
beliefs about human
architecture, cultural values,

00:15:31.850 --> 00:15:33.620 align:middle line:84%
but even maybe the
outcomes of education.

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:35.287 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we believe
differently about that,

00:15:35.287 --> 00:15:36.760 align:middle line:90%
though not necessarily.

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:39.390 align:middle line:84%
The person who's probably the
best counterpoint to John Dewey

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:43.020 align:middle line:84%
is this guy, Ed Thorndike,
who is a very early

00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:44.380 align:middle line:90%
educational psychologist.

00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:46.690 align:middle line:84%
I often think of him as a
guy who was like, no, no, no,

00:15:46.690 --> 00:15:48.255 align:middle line:90%
pail filling is great.

00:15:48.255 --> 00:15:50.130 align:middle line:84%
A particularly great
thing about pail filling

00:15:50.130 --> 00:15:52.078 align:middle line:84%
is that we can
measure pail filling.

00:15:52.078 --> 00:15:54.120 align:middle line:84%
You can measure the rate
at which you pour things

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:56.100 align:middle line:84%
into people and the rate
at which it falls out

00:15:56.100 --> 00:15:59.020 align:middle line:84%
and how leaky people are and
some of those kinds of things.

00:15:59.020 --> 00:16:00.990 align:middle line:84%
So he was very
involved in testing.

00:16:00.990 --> 00:16:04.080 align:middle line:84%
He actually played a
really important role

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:08.100 align:middle line:84%
in developing ideas that
educational progressives

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:08.862 align:middle line:90%
hold dearly.

00:16:08.862 --> 00:16:10.320 align:middle line:84%
One of the things
he studied, which

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:11.945 align:middle line:84%
we'll talk about as
the course goes on,

00:16:11.945 --> 00:16:13.770 align:middle line:90%
is this idea of transfer.

00:16:13.770 --> 00:16:19.110 align:middle line:84%
So in the 18th, 19th century,
people in America or Europe

00:16:19.110 --> 00:16:22.290 align:middle line:84%
believed that a great thing
about learning Latin and Greek

00:16:22.290 --> 00:16:27.220 align:middle line:84%
was it strengthened these kind
of generic thinking muscles.

00:16:27.220 --> 00:16:29.830 align:middle line:84%
Like if you got really good
at learning Latin and Greek,

00:16:29.830 --> 00:16:31.300 align:middle line:84%
you would just be
a good thinker.

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:33.150 align:middle line:90%
And he was like, that's dumb.

00:16:33.150 --> 00:16:35.950 align:middle line:84%
If you learn Latin and Greek,
you're good at Latin and Greek,

00:16:35.950 --> 00:16:38.210 align:middle line:84%
but you're not actually
good at anything else.

00:16:38.210 --> 00:16:40.200 align:middle line:84%
The brain is not general
purpose like that.

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:42.870 align:middle line:84%
But that kind of thinking--
that kind of thinking,

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:46.310 align:middle line:84%
if I learn this thing,
it'll help me generically

00:16:46.310 --> 00:16:48.380 align:middle line:84%
with these other things--
shows up all the time.

00:16:48.380 --> 00:16:50.685 align:middle line:84%
The most contemporary
version of this

00:16:50.685 --> 00:16:52.310 align:middle line:84%
is-- have you ever
encountered the idea

00:16:52.310 --> 00:16:54.110 align:middle line:90%
of computational thinking?

00:16:54.110 --> 00:16:56.330 align:middle line:84%
What people mean by
computational thinking, almost

00:16:56.330 --> 00:16:59.067 align:middle line:84%
all the time, is we're going to
teach you computer programming.

00:16:59.067 --> 00:17:00.900 align:middle line:84%
And if you learn about
computer programming,

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:03.109 align:middle line:84%
you're going to learn
how to break ideas down

00:17:03.109 --> 00:17:04.609 align:middle line:84%
into smaller parts,
and you're going

00:17:04.609 --> 00:17:06.442 align:middle line:84%
to learn how to think
algorithmically and do

00:17:06.442 --> 00:17:07.280 align:middle line:90%
all these other--

00:17:07.280 --> 00:17:08.822 align:middle line:84%
and it's like, no,
you're going to be

00:17:08.822 --> 00:17:10.579 align:middle line:90%
good at programming computers.

00:17:10.579 --> 00:17:13.680 align:middle line:84%
If you want to be able to
break ideas down in poetry,

00:17:13.680 --> 00:17:16.077 align:middle line:84%
learning computer programming
will not help you do that.

00:17:16.077 --> 00:17:17.660 align:middle line:84%
I mean, it will help
you about as much

00:17:17.660 --> 00:17:19.202 align:middle line:84%
as learning Latin
and Greek will help

00:17:19.202 --> 00:17:21.260 align:middle line:84%
you do that, which is
to say, not that much,

00:17:21.260 --> 00:17:23.060 align:middle line:84%
that a frustrating
thing about teaching

00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:26.270 align:middle line:84%
is the things we learn
tend to only help us

00:17:26.270 --> 00:17:29.090 align:middle line:90%
in very near cognate areas.

00:17:29.090 --> 00:17:30.890 align:middle line:90%
So these are the two--

00:17:30.890 --> 00:17:33.470 align:middle line:84%
again, I'm trying to give
you the sense that these two

00:17:33.470 --> 00:17:36.210 align:middle line:90%
big ideas are millennia old.

00:17:36.210 --> 00:17:39.392 align:middle line:84%
They have a scientific basis
that's at least a century old.

00:17:39.392 --> 00:17:40.850 align:middle line:84%
And you're getting
what I think are

00:17:40.850 --> 00:17:44.900 align:middle line:84%
some of the best, most recent
contemporary formulations,

00:17:44.900 --> 00:17:47.420 align:middle line:84%
like the work of John Sweller
and some of these other folks,

00:17:47.420 --> 00:17:50.510 align:middle line:84%
like the little piece you read
from Mitch Resnick for today.

00:17:50.510 --> 00:17:53.270 align:middle line:84%
And then maybe just to give
you a broader overview,

00:17:53.270 --> 00:17:56.162 align:middle line:84%
overwhelmingly,
Thorndike has won.

00:17:56.162 --> 00:17:58.370 align:middle line:84%
If you were to walk through
a typical American school

00:17:58.370 --> 00:18:01.460 align:middle line:84%
on a typical American day, you
would see very little education

00:18:01.460 --> 00:18:05.120 align:middle line:84%
as life itself, and you would
see a lot of direct instruction.

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:08.090 align:middle line:84%
The canonical experience
of students in schools

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:11.130 align:middle line:84%
is very organized around
direct instruction.

00:18:11.130 --> 00:18:13.460 align:middle line:84%
However, if you
were to ask teachers

00:18:13.460 --> 00:18:16.133 align:middle line:84%
who their favorite
educational philosopher is,

00:18:16.133 --> 00:18:18.050 align:middle line:84%
you would get a lot of
answers that are Dewey,

00:18:18.050 --> 00:18:21.290 align:middle line:84%
and you would not get a lot of
answers that were Thorndike.

00:18:21.290 --> 00:18:25.530 align:middle line:84%
So Dewey is beloved in a
way that Thorndike is not,

00:18:25.530 --> 00:18:27.290 align:middle line:84%
but Thorndike is
actually the person

00:18:27.290 --> 00:18:29.170 align:middle line:90%
who inspires more of our ideas.

00:18:29.170 --> 00:18:29.670 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:32.180 align:middle line:84%
Let's do just a little
bit of close reading

00:18:32.180 --> 00:18:34.722 align:middle line:84%
together, which is mostly me
telling you about some things

00:18:34.722 --> 00:18:35.930 align:middle line:90%
that you should read closely.

00:18:35.930 --> 00:18:38.080 align:middle line:84%
This is what we
looked at yesterday.

00:18:38.080 --> 00:18:44.260 align:middle line:84%
One thing that's fun about the
cognitive load theory people

00:18:44.260 --> 00:18:47.950 align:middle line:84%
is that they're really
good at stating clearly

00:18:47.950 --> 00:18:51.080 align:middle line:84%
what their models of human
cognitive architecture are.

00:18:51.080 --> 00:18:54.260 align:middle line:84%
I think the situated learning
people have cognates.

00:18:54.260 --> 00:18:56.590 align:middle line:84%
I think they have
things that they believe

00:18:56.590 --> 00:19:01.330 align:middle line:84%
are central about the
brain, how learning works,

00:19:01.330 --> 00:19:03.160 align:middle line:84%
but they would draw
a different model.

00:19:03.160 --> 00:19:04.535 align:middle line:84%
This is not what
they would draw.

00:19:04.535 --> 00:19:06.610 align:middle line:84%
And they're less explicit
about it, which makes

00:19:06.610 --> 00:19:07.850 align:middle line:90%
our job a little bit harder.

00:19:07.850 --> 00:19:09.910 align:middle line:84%
But a thing that you can
be thinking about now

00:19:09.910 --> 00:19:16.300 align:middle line:84%
is, what is the situated
learning cognate of this?

00:19:16.300 --> 00:19:17.800 align:middle line:84%
What would be the
pieces that they

00:19:17.800 --> 00:19:19.175 align:middle line:84%
would put in the
model that would

00:19:19.175 --> 00:19:21.300 align:middle line:84%
be most important for their
developmental learning?

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:23.342 align:middle line:84%
We'll play with that as
we go along a little bit.

00:19:23.342 --> 00:19:24.160 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:19:24.160 --> 00:19:28.180 align:middle line:84%
How many of you have ever used
the Logo programming language?

00:19:28.180 --> 00:19:29.350 align:middle line:90%
Oh good.

00:19:29.350 --> 00:19:30.940 align:middle line:84%
How many of you
have used Scratch?

00:19:30.940 --> 00:19:32.440 align:middle line:90%
OK, then we got everybody.

00:19:32.440 --> 00:19:34.950 align:middle line:84%
So Scratch is a
descendant of Logo.

00:19:34.950 --> 00:19:37.080 align:middle line:84%
Logo is a programming
language that

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:41.280 align:middle line:84%
was developed by Seymour
Papert in the Media Lab, one

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:46.020 align:middle line:84%
of the first efforts to bring
computer programming to kids

00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:46.900 align:middle line:90%
in schools.

00:19:46.900 --> 00:19:50.227 align:middle line:84%
When I was in the fourth grade
at a Montessori school in South

00:19:50.227 --> 00:19:52.560 align:middle line:84%
Natick, not far from here,
we had a little computer lab,

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:55.020 align:middle line:84%
and Logo was the language
that I learned to do

00:19:55.020 --> 00:19:57.360 align:middle line:90%
a little bit of programming in.

00:19:57.360 --> 00:20:02.190 align:middle line:84%
You'll see that a lot of it
is about shapes and geometry,

00:20:02.190 --> 00:20:05.640 align:middle line:84%
but what I really loved about it
was Choose Your Own Adventures.

00:20:05.640 --> 00:20:10.860 align:middle line:84%
So you could essentially write
a program or function, whatever

00:20:10.860 --> 00:20:13.260 align:middle line:84%
it is, in which
someone types a word

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:17.970 align:middle line:84%
and that triggers the
display of a set of text.

00:20:17.970 --> 00:20:21.660 align:middle line:84%
And so it wasn't that hard to
make Choose Your Own Adventures.

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:23.860 align:middle line:84%
You're at the
front of the house.

00:20:23.860 --> 00:20:25.140 align:middle line:90%
Do you want to walk up?

00:20:25.140 --> 00:20:28.050 align:middle line:84%
Type "porch" if you
want to walk into porch.

00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:30.960 align:middle line:84%
Type "knock" if you want
to knock on the door.

00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:33.700 align:middle line:84%
And then knock is a program that
releases another set of text,

00:20:33.700 --> 00:20:35.580 align:middle line:84%
and you can make a Choose
Your Own Adventure that way.

00:20:35.580 --> 00:20:36.940 align:middle line:84%
How important is
that for you to know?

00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:37.482 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:20:37.482 --> 00:20:42.630 align:middle line:84%
But here is a few minutes
of Seymour Papert, who

00:20:42.630 --> 00:20:45.120 align:middle line:84%
was a student of
Jean Piaget, who's

00:20:45.120 --> 00:20:48.430 align:middle line:84%
another kind of major social
constructivist thinker.

00:20:48.430 --> 00:20:53.100 align:middle line:84%
Here's what Logo was supposed
to look like in the classroom

00:20:53.100 --> 00:20:55.726 align:middle line:84%
in the 1980s, about
when I was a kid.

00:20:55.726 --> 00:20:56.580 align:middle line:90%
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:20:56.580 --> 00:20:59.200 align:middle line:90%
- The idea is quite simple.

00:20:59.200 --> 00:21:01.680 align:middle line:84%
Let a child learn
mathematics by speaking

00:21:01.680 --> 00:21:05.430 align:middle line:84%
in mathematics about things
that really matter to him.

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:10.020 align:middle line:84%
So at MIT, we've given computers
the power to turn motors,

00:21:10.020 --> 00:21:13.050 align:middle line:84%
to make sounds,
to draw pictures,

00:21:13.050 --> 00:21:16.080 align:middle line:84%
and we've found ways of
giving children the power

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:18.520 align:middle line:90%
to control the computer.

00:21:18.520 --> 00:21:21.120 align:middle line:84%
So the child can make
the computer do anything

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:24.390 align:middle line:84%
that he can describe in a
suitable mathematical language

00:21:24.390 --> 00:21:27.005 align:middle line:84%
by typing on a
keyboard like this.

00:21:27.005 --> 00:21:30.540 align:middle line:90%


00:21:30.540 --> 00:21:31.536 align:middle line:90%
- Play it.

00:21:31.536 --> 00:21:33.816 align:middle line:90%
[PLAYFUL TUNE]

00:21:33.816 --> 00:21:34.730 align:middle line:90%


00:21:34.730 --> 00:21:37.440 align:middle line:90%
That's what P1 sounds like.

00:21:37.440 --> 00:21:37.940 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:21:37.940 --> 00:21:38.635 align:middle line:90%
Tap P2.

00:21:38.635 --> 00:21:42.894 align:middle line:90%


00:21:42.894 --> 00:21:45.384 align:middle line:90%
[PLAYFUL TUNE]

00:21:45.384 --> 00:21:45.890 align:middle line:90%


00:21:45.890 --> 00:21:47.000 align:middle line:90%
That's P2.

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:47.620 align:middle line:90%
And P3.

00:21:47.620 --> 00:21:51.876 align:middle line:90%


00:21:51.876 --> 00:21:54.261 align:middle line:90%
[PLAYFUL TUNE]

00:21:54.261 --> 00:21:55.700 align:middle line:90%


00:21:55.700 --> 00:21:58.610 align:middle line:84%
- A computer-controlled turtle
is made to draw and dance

00:21:58.610 --> 00:22:01.670 align:middle line:84%
by programs so simple that
all children invent them

00:22:01.670 --> 00:22:03.270 align:middle line:90%
in the first days of the course.

00:22:03.270 --> 00:22:06.170 align:middle line:84%
Thus, the child acquires
a taste of math power

00:22:06.170 --> 00:22:09.540 align:middle line:84%
that grows and grows as his
projects become more original,

00:22:09.540 --> 00:22:11.930 align:middle line:90%
more complex, and more varied.

00:22:11.930 --> 00:22:15.630 align:middle line:84%
- So you have the first
one, then the second one,

00:22:15.630 --> 00:22:18.800 align:middle line:84%
then the third one, then the
first one again for the ending.

00:22:18.800 --> 00:22:19.565 align:middle line:90%
- OK.

00:22:19.565 --> 00:22:21.440 align:middle line:84%
- Now they will put the
little tunes together

00:22:21.440 --> 00:22:24.356 align:middle line:90%
to make a bigger one.

00:22:24.356 --> 00:22:27.709 align:middle line:90%
[LONGER PLAYFUL TUNE]

00:22:27.709 --> 00:22:32.810 align:middle line:90%


00:22:32.810 --> 00:22:33.310 align:middle line:90%
- Try it.

00:22:33.310 --> 00:22:38.470 align:middle line:90%


00:22:38.470 --> 00:22:40.340 align:middle line:90%
- Now I'm telling it to do it.

00:22:40.340 --> 00:22:45.310 align:middle line:90%


00:22:45.310 --> 00:22:47.710 align:middle line:90%
Oh no.

00:22:47.710 --> 00:22:52.990 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes I get into the
box like this and it'll--

00:22:52.990 --> 00:22:55.340 align:middle line:84%
when it was doing
it in his mind,

00:22:55.340 --> 00:23:02.660 align:middle line:84%
the little turtle, the little
triangle thing, was on an angle.

00:23:02.660 --> 00:23:06.820 align:middle line:84%
So it drew the
picture on an angle.

00:23:06.820 --> 00:23:10.180 align:middle line:84%
So now I'll go back and
have it draw it in its mind

00:23:10.180 --> 00:23:12.680 align:middle line:84%
again while it's in
the right position.

00:23:12.680 --> 00:23:15.080 align:middle line:84%
- And so the child doesn't
have to be told by a teacher

00:23:15.080 --> 00:23:16.520 align:middle line:90%
whether he's right or wrong.

00:23:16.520 --> 00:23:19.270 align:middle line:84%
He can see for himself
whether it works.

00:23:19.270 --> 00:23:22.100 align:middle line:84%
That's what science
and knowledge is about.

00:23:22.100 --> 00:23:24.740 align:middle line:84%
- So this procedure
is named Fancy.

00:23:24.740 --> 00:23:26.920 align:middle line:84%
And then you have
an input, which

00:23:26.920 --> 00:23:29.460 align:middle line:90%
means you can vary the size.

00:23:29.460 --> 00:23:34.560 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to make it 100.

00:23:34.560 --> 00:23:39.720 align:middle line:84%
Right now it's doing a
procedure called Top.

00:23:39.720 --> 00:23:43.900 align:middle line:84%
And each one of those
little fins is called Fin.

00:23:43.900 --> 00:23:46.920 align:middle line:84%
And that's another
subprocedure on Top.

00:23:46.920 --> 00:23:49.635 align:middle line:90%
Now it's doing Inside.

00:23:49.635 --> 00:23:51.900 align:middle line:84%
- There are
identifiable techniques

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:53.260 align:middle line:90%
for working on a problem.

00:23:53.260 --> 00:23:55.260 align:middle line:84%
One of these is known
by these children

00:23:55.260 --> 00:23:58.480 align:middle line:84%
as writing procedures
as subprocedures.

00:23:58.480 --> 00:24:02.850 align:middle line:84%
- Inside and Top are the
two main subprocedures.

00:24:02.850 --> 00:24:08.040 align:middle line:84%
And the other one, the tail
is made out of odds and ends,

00:24:08.040 --> 00:24:10.590 align:middle line:90%
just put together.

00:24:10.590 --> 00:24:12.540 align:middle line:84%
- The technique's
very well known.

00:24:12.540 --> 00:24:14.320 align:middle line:90%
Subdivide the problem.

00:24:14.320 --> 00:24:16.200 align:middle line:90%
Split the difficulties.

00:24:16.200 --> 00:24:18.497 align:middle line:84%
Make a procedure out
of subprocedures.

00:24:18.497 --> 00:24:19.080 align:middle line:90%
Make a whole--

00:24:19.080 --> 00:24:19.410 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:24:19.410 --> 00:24:21.202 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: I'll just
tell you one second

00:24:21.202 --> 00:24:23.110 align:middle line:84%
about this little
robot, which, again,

00:24:23.110 --> 00:24:24.610 align:middle line:90%
is iconic to my childhood.

00:24:24.610 --> 00:24:27.240 align:middle line:84%
I had a commercialized
version of this.

00:24:27.240 --> 00:24:30.442 align:middle line:84%
But it takes the same commands
and uses the same language

00:24:30.442 --> 00:24:31.900 align:middle line:84%
as the turtle that's
on the screen.

00:24:31.900 --> 00:24:33.190 align:middle line:90%
So you tell it Forward 10.

00:24:33.190 --> 00:24:34.440 align:middle line:84%
And there's some
equivalent to Forward 10

00:24:34.440 --> 00:24:35.740 align:middle line:84%
that also shows
up on the screen.

00:24:35.740 --> 00:24:37.865 align:middle line:84%
It has a pen that's mounted
in it that's controlled

00:24:37.865 --> 00:24:39.600 align:middle line:90%
with Pen Up and Pen Down.

00:24:39.600 --> 00:24:42.427 align:middle line:84%
So you can make varying
kinds of shapes using that.

00:24:42.427 --> 00:24:44.760 align:middle line:84%
And then if you see the cable
that the person is walking

00:24:44.760 --> 00:24:46.330 align:middle line:84%
around with, that
was a serial cable,

00:24:46.330 --> 00:24:48.913 align:middle line:84%
which is the primary thing we
used to use to connect computers

00:24:48.913 --> 00:24:49.540 align:middle line:90%
to peripherals.

00:24:49.540 --> 00:24:53.405 align:middle line:84%
So before wireless or
before USB or other things,

00:24:53.405 --> 00:24:54.780 align:middle line:84%
that was the cable
that they were

00:24:54.780 --> 00:24:56.620 align:middle line:90%
using to move things around.

00:24:56.620 --> 00:24:57.390 align:middle line:90%
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:24:57.390 --> 00:24:58.140 align:middle line:90%
- --subprocedures.

00:24:58.140 --> 00:24:59.620 align:middle line:90%
Make a whole out of parts.

00:24:59.620 --> 00:25:01.380 align:middle line:90%
Understand each separately.

00:25:01.380 --> 00:25:05.460 align:middle line:84%
Everybody knows that,
but they don't do it.

00:25:05.460 --> 00:25:07.980 align:middle line:84%
- Now it's moving across
the screen the way

00:25:07.980 --> 00:25:11.880 align:middle line:84%
I told you it did it
before, but this time, this

00:25:11.880 --> 00:25:15.130 align:middle line:84%
is the way I taught myself
to get it across the screen.

00:25:15.130 --> 00:25:17.290 align:middle line:84%
It's the first
time I had done it.

00:25:17.290 --> 00:25:20.520 align:middle line:84%
And so the way I had
the little one to go

00:25:20.520 --> 00:25:22.770 align:middle line:84%
is every time it
moves up a little,

00:25:22.770 --> 00:25:26.180 align:middle line:84%
it subtracts a number, a
little bit of a number.

00:25:26.180 --> 00:25:29.460 align:middle line:90%
And then it's a small number.

00:25:29.460 --> 00:25:33.230 align:middle line:84%
And when it's a certain number,
I tell it that it should stop.

00:25:33.230 --> 00:25:35.330 align:middle line:84%
- Martha is getting
mathematical knowledge

00:25:35.330 --> 00:25:39.350 align:middle line:84%
in a meaningful
context for use now.

00:25:39.350 --> 00:25:42.140 align:middle line:84%
- It just told me I can't
move off the screen.

00:25:42.140 --> 00:25:44.720 align:middle line:84%
- When I was at
school, they told

00:25:44.720 --> 00:25:46.820 align:middle line:84%
me to learn long
division because I

00:25:46.820 --> 00:25:48.740 align:middle line:90%
would need it when I grew up.

00:25:48.740 --> 00:25:52.070 align:middle line:90%
And I think I knew it was a lie.

00:25:52.070 --> 00:25:54.080 align:middle line:84%
I think it was to
make me despise

00:25:54.080 --> 00:25:56.480 align:middle line:90%
the teachers a little more.

00:25:56.480 --> 00:26:03.230 align:middle line:84%
- Now it's telling me
to give a subprocedure.

00:26:03.230 --> 00:26:05.480 align:middle line:90%
Now the bottom of the fish.

00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:10.170 align:middle line:84%
- Steven is using the ideas
of angle, of subprocedure,

00:26:10.170 --> 00:26:14.040 align:middle line:84%
of arc, and of circle in a
confident, purposeful way.

00:26:14.040 --> 00:26:16.490 align:middle line:84%
- The tail, the triangle
part, is made out

00:26:16.490 --> 00:26:19.400 align:middle line:90%
of a procedure called Try 1.

00:26:19.400 --> 00:26:26.510 align:middle line:84%
First you've got to do Right
16 and Forward 50, Back 50.

00:26:26.510 --> 00:26:29.890 align:middle line:90%
Left 60, Forward 50, Back 50.

00:26:29.890 --> 00:26:35.830 align:middle line:84%
Left 60, Forward 50, Back
50, and so on and so forth.

00:26:35.830 --> 00:26:39.070 align:middle line:90%
Now it's doing a Firm.

00:26:39.070 --> 00:26:43.930 align:middle line:84%
That's a radius of
300 and an arc of 90.

00:26:43.930 --> 00:26:45.940 align:middle line:90%
I made a big radius.

00:26:45.940 --> 00:26:51.040 align:middle line:84%
And then I went left,
which will go right over it

00:26:51.040 --> 00:26:52.340 align:middle line:90%
and bring it back.

00:26:52.340 --> 00:26:56.480 align:middle line:84%
And then I made a smaller
radius of the same arc.

00:26:56.480 --> 00:26:59.380 align:middle line:90%
And then it would do that again.

00:26:59.380 --> 00:27:01.010 align:middle line:90%
Then it's an even smaller one.

00:27:01.010 --> 00:27:01.970 align:middle line:90%
It will do that again.

00:27:01.970 --> 00:27:05.320 align:middle line:90%
And that's how I got the fern.

00:27:05.320 --> 00:27:06.940 align:middle line:90%
There we are.

00:27:06.940 --> 00:27:10.690 align:middle line:90%
There's the finished picture.

00:27:10.690 --> 00:27:14.300 align:middle line:84%
- I've made a big song, and
I used that for part of it.

00:27:14.300 --> 00:27:19.360 align:middle line:84%
And then I made an accompaniment
with it with the drum.

00:27:19.360 --> 00:27:20.100 align:middle line:90%
And that's--

00:27:20.100 --> 00:27:30.220 align:middle line:90%


00:27:30.220 --> 00:27:33.650 align:middle line:90%
[PLAYFUL TUNE]

00:27:33.650 --> 00:27:45.430 align:middle line:90%


00:27:45.430 --> 00:27:47.860 align:middle line:84%
- The kids tell me
that's a spiral.

00:27:47.860 --> 00:27:50.440 align:middle line:84%
And I think that was a new
mathematical word that was born

00:27:50.440 --> 00:27:52.270 align:middle line:90%
there that's going to stick.

00:27:52.270 --> 00:27:56.725 align:middle line:90%
I'd call that real discovery.

00:27:56.725 --> 00:27:58.210 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:27:58.210 --> 00:27:59.260 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:27:59.260 --> 00:28:00.738 align:middle line:90%
We won't spend time with this.

00:28:00.738 --> 00:28:02.030 align:middle line:90%
Turn to the people next to you.

00:28:02.030 --> 00:28:02.630 align:middle line:90%
Talk to them a little bit.

00:28:02.630 --> 00:28:04.120 align:middle line:84%
What are the things
that you saw in there

00:28:04.120 --> 00:28:05.620 align:middle line:84%
that cohere with
ideas that you read

00:28:05.620 --> 00:28:07.880 align:middle line:84%
about from other communities
of situated learning?

00:28:07.880 --> 00:28:09.255 align:middle line:84%
What are some
things that you saw

00:28:09.255 --> 00:28:11.440 align:middle line:84%
in there that cohered with
cognitive load theory?

00:28:11.440 --> 00:28:14.290 align:middle line:84%
Do you see evidence
of direct instruction

00:28:14.290 --> 00:28:15.907 align:middle line:90%
that was in there anywhere?

00:28:15.907 --> 00:28:17.740 align:middle line:84%
And then, what kind of
learning environments

00:28:17.740 --> 00:28:21.057 align:middle line:84%
have you been, where you
learned in this kind of way that

00:28:21.057 --> 00:28:23.640 align:middle line:84%
was pleasurable, and have people
been in learning environments

00:28:23.640 --> 00:28:25.807 align:middle line:84%
where you've learned in
this way that you found very

00:28:25.807 --> 00:28:27.210 align:middle line:90%
unpleasurable and unlikable?

00:28:27.210 --> 00:28:28.180 align:middle line:84%
Discuss that for
a couple minutes.

00:28:28.180 --> 00:28:28.680 align:middle line:90%
Ready?

00:28:28.680 --> 00:28:29.795 align:middle line:90%
Go.

00:28:29.795 --> 00:28:30.670 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Every time.

00:28:30.670 --> 00:28:31.110 align:middle line:90%
It's awesome.

00:28:31.110 --> 00:28:31.902 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:28:31.902 --> 00:28:33.880 align:middle line:84%
What are some connections
that people made?

00:28:33.880 --> 00:28:36.510 align:middle line:84%
What are some
things where you saw

00:28:36.510 --> 00:28:40.030 align:middle line:84%
examples of situated learning
or cognitive load theory,

00:28:40.030 --> 00:28:44.730 align:middle line:84%
or things that seem
pleasurable or frustrating?

00:28:44.730 --> 00:28:46.890 align:middle line:84%
What did you talk
about in your groups?

00:28:46.890 --> 00:28:50.290 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: We were talking about
learning programming in general,

00:28:50.290 --> 00:28:53.118 align:middle line:84%
or it seems like you always need
some base level of whether it's

00:28:53.118 --> 00:28:55.410 align:middle line:84%
the instructions or knowing
whatever commands there are

00:28:55.410 --> 00:28:58.110 align:middle line:84%
in Logo, and that most
likely comes from more

00:28:58.110 --> 00:28:59.440 align:middle line:90%
of an instructional place.

00:28:59.440 --> 00:29:01.720 align:middle line:84%
But then you can get
into these situations,

00:29:01.720 --> 00:29:03.970 align:middle line:84%
like we saw in the video,
where it becomes open world.

00:29:03.970 --> 00:29:05.970 align:middle line:84%
But it still seems like
you need some base level

00:29:05.970 --> 00:29:07.170 align:middle line:90%
where CLT needs to come in.

00:29:07.170 --> 00:29:09.870 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, or some
kind of direct instruction.

00:29:09.870 --> 00:29:15.450 align:middle line:84%
What evidence of direct
instruction did people see?

00:29:15.450 --> 00:29:17.880 align:middle line:84%
Where were you like, eh, that
kid didn't figure that out

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:18.640 align:middle line:90%
on their own.

00:29:18.640 --> 00:29:20.320 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I mean,
just the terminology.

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:22.610 align:middle line:84%
They figured out procedure,
subprocedure, all

00:29:22.610 --> 00:29:25.115 align:middle line:90%
of these things hard phrases.

00:29:25.115 --> 00:29:27.210 align:middle line:84%
A kid wouldn't just
figure this out.

00:29:27.210 --> 00:29:27.320 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:29:27.320 --> 00:29:27.570 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:29:27.570 --> 00:29:30.050 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't seem like Steven came
up with the term "procedure"

00:29:30.050 --> 00:29:30.842 align:middle line:90%
and "subprocedure."

00:29:30.842 --> 00:29:34.070 align:middle line:84%
Pretty sure somebody taught
him, sat down, and said,

00:29:34.070 --> 00:29:35.780 align:middle line:84%
here are a couple of
useful definitions

00:29:35.780 --> 00:29:37.780 align:middle line:84%
that will allow us to
communicate with this more

00:29:37.780 --> 00:29:38.308 align:middle line:90%
clearly.

00:29:38.308 --> 00:29:40.850 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Also, we were talking
about when the two girls were

00:29:40.850 --> 00:29:41.767 align:middle line:90%
talking to each other.

00:29:41.767 --> 00:29:45.090 align:middle line:84%
It was like one at the beginning
was instructing the other

00:29:45.090 --> 00:29:46.320 align:middle line:90%
to actually make the music.

00:29:46.320 --> 00:29:46.430 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:29:46.430 --> 00:29:47.330 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So I guess
that's kind of--

00:29:47.330 --> 00:29:49.050 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Oh, very
direct instruction.

00:29:49.050 --> 00:29:49.350 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:29:49.350 --> 00:29:51.750 align:middle line:84%
Direct instruction within a
situated learning environment.

00:29:51.750 --> 00:29:51.860 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:29:51.860 --> 00:29:52.110 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:29:52.110 --> 00:29:53.180 align:middle line:90%
I mean it's-- yeah, yeah.

00:29:53.180 --> 00:29:55.130 align:middle line:90%
It's a kind of worked example.

00:29:55.130 --> 00:29:58.638 align:middle line:84%
Oh, I'm going to show
you how I made a thing.

00:29:58.638 --> 00:30:00.680 align:middle line:84%
We're going to make this
thing together right now

00:30:00.680 --> 00:30:02.790 align:middle line:84%
by, first of all, play
each of the pieces.

00:30:02.790 --> 00:30:04.300 align:middle line:84%
Now we're going to play
all the pieces together.

00:30:04.300 --> 00:30:05.800 align:middle line:84%
I've already learned
how to do that.

00:30:05.800 --> 00:30:08.210 align:middle line:84%
What makes it a kind
of peer learning,

00:30:08.210 --> 00:30:10.690 align:middle line:84%
situated learning kind of
thing is it's one young person

00:30:10.690 --> 00:30:11.940 align:middle line:90%
teaching it to another person.

00:30:11.940 --> 00:30:15.070 align:middle line:84%
But it does seem to be
direct instruction, for sure.

00:30:15.070 --> 00:30:17.570 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: That kind of led us
into a conversation about how,

00:30:17.570 --> 00:30:20.780 align:middle line:84%
within situated
learning, that there's

00:30:20.780 --> 00:30:23.930 align:middle line:84%
two ways that it can be seen
as you have someone who's

00:30:23.930 --> 00:30:27.110 align:middle line:84%
an old timer, like the
other girl explaining

00:30:27.110 --> 00:30:31.340 align:middle line:84%
to the other girl
about the situation

00:30:31.340 --> 00:30:32.580 align:middle line:90%
that they were working with.

00:30:32.580 --> 00:30:34.460 align:middle line:84%
But then there's
also the instances

00:30:34.460 --> 00:30:38.210 align:middle line:84%
when it was just the student and
the computer and that you lost

00:30:38.210 --> 00:30:41.960 align:middle line:84%
that social aspect that you have
of learning from another person.

00:30:41.960 --> 00:30:44.570 align:middle line:84%
And then we went into an
interesting conversation about

00:30:44.570 --> 00:30:48.710 align:middle line:84%
whether maybe the student was
having a social interaction

00:30:48.710 --> 00:30:51.743 align:middle line:84%
from themself and learning
with the computer as a tool,

00:30:51.743 --> 00:30:53.660 align:middle line:84%
or maybe they were having
a social interaction

00:30:53.660 --> 00:30:55.290 align:middle line:84%
with the computer
itself and they

00:30:55.290 --> 00:30:57.290 align:middle line:84%
were learning from the
computer and the computer

00:30:57.290 --> 00:31:01.278 align:middle line:84%
was learning what they really
wanted the outcome to be.

00:31:01.278 --> 00:31:02.070 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:31:02.070 --> 00:31:02.670 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, good.

00:31:02.670 --> 00:31:03.920 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, that's
definitely something

00:31:03.920 --> 00:31:05.503 align:middle line:84%
we'll think about
more as we go along.

00:31:05.503 --> 00:31:07.590 align:middle line:84%
What kind of agent
is the machine?

00:31:07.590 --> 00:31:09.970 align:middle line:84%
What kind of relationship
do we have to the machines?

00:31:09.970 --> 00:31:11.220 align:middle line:90%
How does the machine motivate?

00:31:11.220 --> 00:31:12.440 align:middle line:90%
How does the machine explain?

00:31:12.440 --> 00:31:14.623 align:middle line:84%
The thing that seems
pretty rad here,

00:31:14.623 --> 00:31:16.040 align:middle line:84%
that Seymour Papert
is telling us,

00:31:16.040 --> 00:31:19.600 align:middle line:84%
is that the machine gives some
great feedback, although lots

00:31:19.600 --> 00:31:21.040 align:middle line:90%
of other things do.

00:31:21.040 --> 00:31:23.090 align:middle line:90%
Hand drills give great feedback.

00:31:23.090 --> 00:31:25.550 align:middle line:84%
When you go through
the wall, you know it.

00:31:25.550 --> 00:31:27.890 align:middle line:84%
And when you hit something
metal or something like that,

00:31:27.890 --> 00:31:28.390 align:middle line:90%
you know it.

00:31:28.390 --> 00:31:30.460 align:middle line:84%
And you get that kind
of instant feedback.

00:31:30.460 --> 00:31:32.710 align:middle line:84%
There are other things
that we teach people

00:31:32.710 --> 00:31:36.130 align:middle line:84%
where it's just hard to imagine
where that instant feedback

00:31:36.130 --> 00:31:37.510 align:middle line:90%
might come from.

00:31:37.510 --> 00:31:40.730 align:middle line:84%
Writing is often that way,
especially writing other people.

00:31:40.730 --> 00:31:42.380 align:middle line:90%
There's nothing that is--

00:31:42.380 --> 00:31:44.650 align:middle line:84%
we haven't built things very
well where a machine can

00:31:44.650 --> 00:31:46.420 align:middle line:84%
tell you, oh, that's
a compelling way

00:31:46.420 --> 00:31:49.390 align:middle line:84%
of expressing that idea, in the
same way that I can be like,

00:31:49.390 --> 00:31:51.700 align:middle line:90%
oh, my swan is on its side.

00:31:51.700 --> 00:31:57.640 align:middle line:84%
So different technologies and
different things that we study

00:31:57.640 --> 00:32:00.610 align:middle line:84%
have different capacity to give
us immediate feedback of what

00:32:00.610 --> 00:32:02.740 align:middle line:90%
we're doing.

00:32:02.740 --> 00:32:03.310 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:32:03.310 --> 00:32:04.935 align:middle line:84%
Has anybody ever been
in an environment

00:32:04.935 --> 00:32:07.692 align:middle line:84%
like this, where you're like,
oh my gosh, this is the worst?

00:32:07.692 --> 00:32:10.150 align:middle line:84%
Anyone have experience in this
kind of open-ended learning,

00:32:10.150 --> 00:32:12.980 align:middle line:84%
where you're like,
this is terrible?

00:32:12.980 --> 00:32:14.870 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I
remember this one--

00:32:14.870 --> 00:32:17.260 align:middle line:84%
I guess this is a more
recent MIT experience,

00:32:17.260 --> 00:32:19.980 align:middle line:84%
where basically one of
the classes' final project

00:32:19.980 --> 00:32:21.150 align:middle line:90%
was very open ended.

00:32:21.150 --> 00:32:23.740 align:middle line:84%
And they were like, oh,
just design this game.

00:32:23.740 --> 00:32:25.027 align:middle line:90%
And I was like, wow.

00:32:25.027 --> 00:32:26.610 align:middle line:84%
It would be nice to
get some kind of--

00:32:26.610 --> 00:32:27.270 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah,
will you please

00:32:27.270 --> 00:32:29.280 align:middle line:90%
show me how to design this game?

00:32:29.280 --> 00:32:31.030 align:middle line:84%
Someone has figured
this out before.

00:32:31.030 --> 00:32:33.835 align:middle line:84%
I don't want to explore
this space forever.

00:32:33.835 --> 00:32:36.210 align:middle line:84%
No, I've definitely had people
who have taken this class,

00:32:36.210 --> 00:32:38.752 align:middle line:84%
and they watch this video, and
they're like, give me a break.

00:32:38.752 --> 00:32:41.760 align:middle line:84%
Just show me how
to use the turtle.

00:32:41.760 --> 00:32:45.210 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I would say, in high
school, a lot of the AP Computer

00:32:45.210 --> 00:32:49.600 align:middle line:84%
Science classes are very into
this kind of situated learning.

00:32:49.600 --> 00:32:52.800 align:middle line:84%
But then, if you have
certain classrooms where--

00:32:52.800 --> 00:32:55.650 align:middle line:84%
in my instance, it was
just overloaded with people

00:32:55.650 --> 00:32:56.850 align:middle line:90%
who just needed an elective.

00:32:56.850 --> 00:32:58.950 align:middle line:84%
And they were like, you
have to take this class.

00:32:58.950 --> 00:33:04.440 align:middle line:84%
Then it becomes-- that
peer-to-peer interaction becomes

00:33:04.440 --> 00:33:06.120 align:middle line:84%
an issue where
it's like, OK, you

00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:09.930 align:middle line:84%
have three people who are trying
to teach 30 other people how

00:33:09.930 --> 00:33:13.380 align:middle line:84%
to do a specific thing because
you're in the same group

00:33:13.380 --> 00:33:16.120 align:middle line:84%
and they have to pass, and
they don't really care anyway.

00:33:16.120 --> 00:33:16.320 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:33:16.320 --> 00:33:17.550 align:middle line:84%
People have different
motivations.

00:33:17.550 --> 00:33:19.950 align:middle line:84%
I mean, a huge problem that
computer science education

00:33:19.950 --> 00:33:20.950 align:middle line:90%
has is that people--

00:33:20.950 --> 00:33:22.617 align:middle line:84%
unlike other subjects,
people come to it

00:33:22.617 --> 00:33:25.200 align:middle line:84%
with very different levels
of background experience.

00:33:25.200 --> 00:33:27.870 align:middle line:84%
So none of you probably
ever took a geology class

00:33:27.870 --> 00:33:30.730 align:middle line:84%
where a third of the kids were
like, man, I just love geology.

00:33:30.730 --> 00:33:32.947 align:middle line:84%
I do geology all the
time in my spare time.

00:33:32.947 --> 00:33:34.030 align:middle line:90%
I'm way ahead of you guys.

00:33:34.030 --> 00:33:35.820 align:middle line:84%
But that is essentially what
just about every computer

00:33:35.820 --> 00:33:38.400 align:middle line:84%
science class we teach is
like, where a chunk of people

00:33:38.400 --> 00:33:41.180 align:middle line:84%
are way into it and know
a bunch more already.

00:33:41.180 --> 00:33:42.930 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, those
open-ended environments

00:33:42.930 --> 00:33:46.770 align:middle line:84%
can be really great
for those folks who

00:33:46.770 --> 00:33:48.240 align:middle line:84%
have that extra
expertise, and they

00:33:48.240 --> 00:33:52.330 align:middle line:84%
can be a real bummer for people
who can't jump in as naturally.

00:33:52.330 --> 00:33:54.210 align:middle line:84%
Harmon, what were
you going to say?

00:33:54.210 --> 00:33:57.720 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think what this
made me think of is how CLT

00:33:57.720 --> 00:34:01.470 align:middle line:84%
and situated learning cannot
be really separated from each

00:34:01.470 --> 00:34:02.050 align:middle line:90%
other.

00:34:02.050 --> 00:34:06.010 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes you need to CLT to
give framework for students,

00:34:06.010 --> 00:34:08.699 align:middle line:84%
and then from that,
I guess, you can fire

00:34:08.699 --> 00:34:09.824 align:middle line:90%
the wood and the situated--

00:34:09.824 --> 00:34:10.616 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:34:10.616 --> 00:34:11.190 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:34:11.190 --> 00:34:12.280 align:middle line:90%
I like it, I like it.

00:34:12.280 --> 00:34:12.389 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:34:12.389 --> 00:34:12.699 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:34:12.699 --> 00:34:14.949 align:middle line:84%
What are the-- I think that's
a great question to ask.

00:34:14.949 --> 00:34:17.690 align:middle line:84%
What are the different
combination and permutations--

00:34:17.690 --> 00:34:19.650 align:middle line:84%
the reason why these
ideas have existed,

00:34:19.650 --> 00:34:22.040 align:middle line:84%
that we can find evidence
of them for 2,000 years,

00:34:22.040 --> 00:34:24.590 align:middle line:84%
is that they both
seem pretty good.

00:34:24.590 --> 00:34:28.592 align:middle line:84%
They both seem useful
in different ways.

00:34:28.592 --> 00:34:32.300 align:middle line:84%
Seymour Papert is once talking
about his teachers lying to them

00:34:32.300 --> 00:34:33.690 align:middle line:90%
and despising schools.

00:34:33.690 --> 00:34:35.510 align:middle line:84%
But you can also
find school things,

00:34:35.510 --> 00:34:38.000 align:middle line:84%
like we're going to need some
definitions that I teach you

00:34:38.000 --> 00:34:41.780 align:middle line:84%
that are kind of woven even
in this extreme example.

00:34:41.780 --> 00:34:43.760 align:middle line:84%
Let me give you a few
pieces from the thing

00:34:43.760 --> 00:34:46.650 align:middle line:84%
that you read from a guy
named Jean Lave, who,

00:34:46.650 --> 00:34:48.830 align:middle line:84%
with another person
named Etienne Wenger,

00:34:48.830 --> 00:34:51.409 align:middle line:84%
really advanced
some of these ideas.

00:34:51.409 --> 00:34:53.900 align:middle line:84%
And I want you to
be thinking about,

00:34:53.900 --> 00:34:56.360 align:middle line:84%
so how is it like--
what are the methods

00:34:56.360 --> 00:34:58.020 align:middle line:90%
that this person uses to study?

00:34:58.020 --> 00:34:59.850 align:middle line:84%
What are the kind of
arguments they make?

00:34:59.850 --> 00:35:01.770 align:middle line:84%
What are the implications
for pedagogy?

00:35:01.770 --> 00:35:05.730 align:middle line:84%
What's their framework about
human beings and how they learn?

00:35:05.730 --> 00:35:08.140 align:middle line:84%
And where do these
things lap and overlap?

00:35:08.140 --> 00:35:10.820 align:middle line:84%
It can often seem like
when you're writing about--

00:35:10.820 --> 00:35:13.070 align:middle line:84%
if you read a bunch of stuff
on cognitive load theory,

00:35:13.070 --> 00:35:15.230 align:middle line:84%
it seems like they're just
talking about instruction,

00:35:15.230 --> 00:35:16.690 align:middle line:84%
and they mostly
are, but there are

00:35:16.690 --> 00:35:18.220 align:middle line:84%
ways that talking
about instruction

00:35:18.220 --> 00:35:20.950 align:middle line:84%
that evoke their beliefs
about culture, that

00:35:20.950 --> 00:35:23.410 align:middle line:84%
evoke their beliefs
about values, that

00:35:23.410 --> 00:35:25.495 align:middle line:84%
evoke their beliefs around
methods and outcomes,

00:35:25.495 --> 00:35:27.620 align:middle line:84%
even if they don't talk
about those things as much.

00:35:27.620 --> 00:35:30.700 align:middle line:84%
"In this chapter, I propose
to consider learning not

00:35:30.700 --> 00:35:34.450 align:middle line:84%
as a process of socially
shared cognition that results

00:35:34.450 --> 00:35:37.120 align:middle line:84%
in the end in the
internalization of knowledge

00:35:37.120 --> 00:35:41.020 align:middle line:84%
by individuals, but as a
process of becoming a member

00:35:41.020 --> 00:35:43.900 align:middle line:84%
of a sustained
community of practice."

00:35:43.900 --> 00:35:47.620 align:middle line:84%
So anytime you read anything,
a thing that you are almost all

00:35:47.620 --> 00:35:49.930 align:middle line:84%
probably trained to do
is try to figure out,

00:35:49.930 --> 00:35:51.557 align:middle line:84%
what is the person's
core argument?

00:35:51.557 --> 00:35:53.140 align:middle line:84%
What is the thing
they're arguing for?

00:35:53.140 --> 00:35:55.338 align:middle line:84%
An equally useful thing
to do, almost any time

00:35:55.338 --> 00:35:57.130 align:middle line:84%
you read anything, is
to try to figure out,

00:35:57.130 --> 00:35:59.440 align:middle line:84%
what is this person
arguing against?

00:35:59.440 --> 00:36:02.590 align:middle line:84%
And people are often
not that explicit.

00:36:02.590 --> 00:36:05.860 align:middle line:84%
He's not saying,
these jerks who are

00:36:05.860 --> 00:36:08.500 align:middle line:84%
really big on schools
and direct instruction

00:36:08.500 --> 00:36:09.980 align:middle line:90%
have totally missed this stuff.

00:36:09.980 --> 00:36:12.880 align:middle line:84%
But that's kind of what
he's getting at in here.

00:36:12.880 --> 00:36:14.530 align:middle line:90%
And it's a radical statement.

00:36:14.530 --> 00:36:17.350 align:middle line:84%
Learning is not the
internalization of knowledge.

00:36:17.350 --> 00:36:19.050 align:middle line:84%
Last week, I told
you that, for some

00:36:19.050 --> 00:36:21.850 align:middle line:84%
of these kind of educational
psychology people,

00:36:21.850 --> 00:36:23.460 align:middle line:84%
memory is the
residue of thought.

00:36:23.460 --> 00:36:25.800 align:middle line:84%
The entire endpoint of
cognitive load theory

00:36:25.800 --> 00:36:30.630 align:middle line:84%
is to store facts as memories
in our long-term memory.

00:36:30.630 --> 00:36:32.940 align:middle line:84%
"Not as the internalization
of knowledge

00:36:32.940 --> 00:36:34.920 align:middle line:84%
as individuals, but as
a process of becoming

00:36:34.920 --> 00:36:37.450 align:middle line:84%
a member of a sustained
community of practice.

00:36:37.450 --> 00:36:40.230 align:middle line:84%
Developing an identity as
a member of a community

00:36:40.230 --> 00:36:42.000 align:middle line:84%
and becoming
knowledgeable and skillful

00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:44.110 align:middle line:90%
are part of the same process.

00:36:44.110 --> 00:36:47.850 align:middle line:84%
You cannot separate storing
things in long-term memory from

00:36:47.850 --> 00:36:50.040 align:middle line:84%
building relationships
with other people,

00:36:50.040 --> 00:36:53.700 align:middle line:84%
with the former motivating"--
one of the key things that we

00:36:53.700 --> 00:36:55.590 align:middle line:84%
remember our colleague
Michael Pershan,

00:36:55.590 --> 00:36:58.260 align:middle line:84%
not a theory of everything,
where is the motivation?--

00:36:58.260 --> 00:37:00.180 align:middle line:84%
"with the former
motivating, shaping,

00:37:00.180 --> 00:37:02.620 align:middle line:84%
and giving meaning to the
latter, which it subsumes.

00:37:02.620 --> 00:37:05.700 align:middle line:84%
It is difficult to move from
peripheral to full participation

00:37:05.700 --> 00:37:08.230 align:middle line:84%
in today's world, including
workplaces and schools,

00:37:08.230 --> 00:37:10.730 align:middle line:84%
thereby developing knowledgeably
skilled identities.

00:37:10.730 --> 00:37:13.180 align:middle line:84%
This is because the processes
by which we divide and sell

00:37:13.180 --> 00:37:15.970 align:middle line:84%
labor, which are ubiquitous
in our way of producing goods

00:37:15.970 --> 00:37:17.480 align:middle line:84%
and services,
including knowledge,

00:37:17.480 --> 00:37:20.918 align:middle line:84%
truncate both the movement from
peripheral to full participation

00:37:20.918 --> 00:37:22.460 align:middle line:84%
and the scope of
knowledgeable skill.

00:37:22.460 --> 00:37:26.350 align:middle line:84%
Taken to an extreme, these
processes separate identity

00:37:26.350 --> 00:37:29.240 align:middle line:84%
from intended forms of
knowledgeable practice.

00:37:29.240 --> 00:37:32.140 align:middle line:84%
This view implies that learning
and failure to learn are aspects

00:37:32.140 --> 00:37:34.220 align:middle line:84%
of the same social
historical processes,

00:37:34.220 --> 00:37:36.400 align:middle line:84%
and points to relationships
between knowledgeability

00:37:36.400 --> 00:37:38.900 align:middle line:84%
and identity as important
focus for research."

00:37:38.900 --> 00:37:41.435 align:middle line:84%
These jerks, these
education psychologists,

00:37:41.435 --> 00:37:43.810 align:middle line:84%
keep trying to convince you
that what learning is is just

00:37:43.810 --> 00:37:45.140 align:middle line:90%
shoving stuff in your brain.

00:37:45.140 --> 00:37:46.010 align:middle line:90%
It's not.

00:37:46.010 --> 00:37:48.327 align:middle line:84%
There are always people
around when we're learning.

00:37:48.327 --> 00:37:49.910 align:middle line:84%
Even when we're
learning by ourselves,

00:37:49.910 --> 00:37:50.960 align:middle line:84%
reading books and
stuff like that,

00:37:50.960 --> 00:37:53.200 align:middle line:84%
there are people in our minds,
in our communities that we

00:37:53.200 --> 00:37:54.460 align:middle line:90%
see ourselves being part of.

00:37:54.460 --> 00:37:58.930 align:middle line:84%
Trying to understand how
learning happens in schools

00:37:58.930 --> 00:38:00.895 align:middle line:84%
or any other
learning environment

00:38:00.895 --> 00:38:02.770 align:middle line:84%
without thinking about
the social environment

00:38:02.770 --> 00:38:04.820 align:middle line:90%
that people learn is bonkers.

00:38:04.820 --> 00:38:06.310 align:middle line:90%
It's nuts.

00:38:06.310 --> 00:38:10.100 align:middle line:84%
It denies a crucial
part of our humanity.

00:38:10.100 --> 00:38:12.710 align:middle line:84%
Here is a blog post that
you can look at later

00:38:12.710 --> 00:38:13.710 align:middle line:90%
if you follow this link.

00:38:13.710 --> 00:38:15.085 align:middle line:84%
It's by a wonderful
math educator

00:38:15.085 --> 00:38:18.890 align:middle line:84%
named Dan Meyer, who has been
trying to explain to people why

00:38:18.890 --> 00:38:23.210 align:middle line:84%
he thinks AI chatbots are a
stupid idea and will not work.

00:38:23.210 --> 00:38:28.560 align:middle line:84%
And his core issue is, nobody
wants to talk to a computer.

00:38:28.560 --> 00:38:32.000 align:middle line:84%
Maybe the computer can provide
a perfect, direct instructional

00:38:32.000 --> 00:38:35.210 align:middle line:84%
sequence, but the kids who
are in my eighth grade algebra

00:38:35.210 --> 00:38:37.200 align:middle line:84%
classroom, they don't
want to be there.

00:38:37.200 --> 00:38:39.290 align:middle line:84%
They're not
interested in algebra.

00:38:39.290 --> 00:38:41.390 align:middle line:90%
I love this framing.

00:38:41.390 --> 00:38:46.340 align:middle line:84%
"A student attends school to
answer two questions-- who am I,

00:38:46.340 --> 00:38:47.828 align:middle line:90%
and how do I do this?"

00:38:47.828 --> 00:38:50.370 align:middle line:84%
Those are the two things they're
thinking about all the time.

00:38:50.370 --> 00:38:52.260 align:middle line:90%
Who am I, and how do I do this?

00:38:52.260 --> 00:38:55.997 align:middle line:84%
A chatbot cannot help you at
all with the first question.

00:38:55.997 --> 00:38:58.080 align:middle line:84%
But the other thing that
I want to bring up here--

00:38:58.080 --> 00:38:59.600 align:middle line:84%
and you can disagree with that,
and we'll talk about it more

00:38:59.600 --> 00:39:00.767 align:middle line:90%
when we talk about chatbots.

00:39:00.767 --> 00:39:03.150 align:middle line:84%
But who am I, and
how do I do this?

00:39:03.150 --> 00:39:05.660 align:middle line:84%
This is exactly what
Jean Lave was discussing,

00:39:05.660 --> 00:39:08.440 align:middle line:84%
that identity cannot be
separated from the acquisition

00:39:08.440 --> 00:39:09.760 align:middle line:90%
of knowledge.

00:39:09.760 --> 00:39:11.433 align:middle line:84%
And I think most
of you probably--

00:39:11.433 --> 00:39:13.600 align:middle line:84%
if you think of your own
classroom experience, think

00:39:13.600 --> 00:39:14.740 align:middle line:90%
of your own school experience.

00:39:14.740 --> 00:39:16.660 align:middle line:84%
I spent a lot of time in middle
school and high school thinking

00:39:16.660 --> 00:39:18.970 align:middle line:84%
to myself, in one form
or another, who am I?

00:39:18.970 --> 00:39:20.127 align:middle line:90%
What do people think of me?

00:39:20.127 --> 00:39:21.710 align:middle line:84%
What kinds of
relationships do I have?

00:39:21.710 --> 00:39:22.250 align:middle line:90%
Why am I here?

00:39:22.250 --> 00:39:23.542 align:middle line:90%
What am I supposed to be doing?

00:39:23.542 --> 00:39:26.170 align:middle line:90%


00:39:26.170 --> 00:39:28.688 align:middle line:84%
"Jordan has carried out
extensive field research

00:39:28.688 --> 00:39:30.730 align:middle line:84%
on the Yucatan Maya midwives
whose apprenticeship

00:39:30.730 --> 00:39:32.207 align:middle line:84%
is quite different--
more effective

00:39:32.207 --> 00:39:34.790 align:middle line:84%
and less exploitative-- than the
butchers of Marshall's study.

00:39:34.790 --> 00:39:37.640 align:middle line:84%
The apprentices are
peripheral participants,

00:39:37.640 --> 00:39:40.560 align:middle line:84%
legitimate participants,
and legitimate peripheral

00:39:40.560 --> 00:39:41.810 align:middle line:90%
to the practice of midwifery."

00:39:41.810 --> 00:39:43.810 align:middle line:84%
What did you all take
away from what you think--

00:39:43.810 --> 00:39:46.660 align:middle line:84%
what is legitimate peripheral
practice or periphery?

00:39:46.660 --> 00:39:49.840 align:middle line:84%
What does that mean
in your own words?

00:39:49.840 --> 00:39:51.010 align:middle line:90%
What do you think, Dana?

00:39:51.010 --> 00:39:53.300 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: They're doing
work that matters,

00:39:53.300 --> 00:39:55.600 align:middle line:84%
but they're not
doing the main work.

00:39:55.600 --> 00:39:58.330 align:middle line:84%
They're doing side tasks that
are helping them understand

00:39:58.330 --> 00:40:01.390 align:middle line:84%
the main process, but
they themselves are not

00:40:01.390 --> 00:40:02.690 align:middle line:90%
giving birth to the child.

00:40:02.690 --> 00:40:02.970 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:40:02.970 --> 00:40:03.845 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:40:03.845 --> 00:40:05.600 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: And
there's a space there.

00:40:05.600 --> 00:40:08.240 align:middle line:84%
A space is held
for those people.

00:40:08.240 --> 00:40:10.480 align:middle line:84%
It is expected that
there are going

00:40:10.480 --> 00:40:12.580 align:middle line:84%
to be some folks who are
hanging around midwives

00:40:12.580 --> 00:40:15.830 align:middle line:84%
who are not quite midwives
yet, and that's normal.

00:40:15.830 --> 00:40:17.440 align:middle line:90%
They're supposed to be there.

00:40:17.440 --> 00:40:19.020 align:middle line:84%
It's not a classroom
of 20 people

00:40:19.020 --> 00:40:21.520 align:middle line:84%
all lined up who are supposed
to be doing the same thing all

00:40:21.520 --> 00:40:22.100 align:middle line:90%
the time.

00:40:22.100 --> 00:40:23.870 align:middle line:84%
There's supposed to be
some people in the middle,

00:40:23.870 --> 00:40:25.940 align:middle line:84%
and there's supposed to be
some people on the outside.

00:40:25.940 --> 00:40:27.607 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I want to
pose a question too,

00:40:27.607 --> 00:40:30.100 align:middle line:84%
to think of the relationship
between what Jean Lave was

00:40:30.100 --> 00:40:32.270 align:middle line:84%
talking about, the
midwives and the butchers,

00:40:32.270 --> 00:40:34.420 align:middle line:84%
and the thinking,
what's the difference

00:40:34.420 --> 00:40:37.330 align:middle line:84%
between peripheral
participants and legitimately

00:40:37.330 --> 00:40:40.840 align:middle line:90%
peripheral participants?

00:40:40.840 --> 00:40:43.640 align:middle line:84%
Do folks remember what they
described about the butchers?

00:40:43.640 --> 00:40:44.265 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:40:44.265 --> 00:40:46.930 align:middle line:84%
It was that the butchers,
even though they

00:40:46.930 --> 00:40:50.440 align:middle line:84%
were doing peripheral work,
they were separated in space

00:40:50.440 --> 00:40:54.640 align:middle line:90%
from the experts.

00:40:54.640 --> 00:40:58.390 align:middle line:84%
So they wouldn't really pick
up on things, as opposed

00:40:58.390 --> 00:41:02.170 align:middle line:84%
to midwives who would still
observe what the legitimate--

00:41:02.170 --> 00:41:03.195 align:middle line:90%
the expert was doing.

00:41:03.195 --> 00:41:03.820 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:41:03.820 --> 00:41:05.550 align:middle line:84%
So what does that
mean in terms of being

00:41:05.550 --> 00:41:08.580 align:middle line:84%
a part of that community,
and then shaping the learning

00:41:08.580 --> 00:41:12.000 align:middle line:90%
that they're able to acquire?

00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:14.380 align:middle line:84%
If I'm a butcher and I'm
part of the community,

00:41:14.380 --> 00:41:16.780 align:middle line:84%
if I'm standing over here
cutting my meat, using my tool,

00:41:16.780 --> 00:41:18.010 align:middle line:90%
that's all I get to do.

00:41:18.010 --> 00:41:21.180 align:middle line:84%
And then Justin's over there,
hanging out with the pig

00:41:21.180 --> 00:41:22.600 align:middle line:90%
or whatever it is, right?

00:41:22.600 --> 00:41:24.793 align:middle line:90%
What am I losing then?

00:41:24.793 --> 00:41:25.960 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: The social aspect.

00:41:25.960 --> 00:41:27.127 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: The social aspect.

00:41:27.127 --> 00:41:28.347 align:middle line:90%
Am I able to observe him?

00:41:28.347 --> 00:41:29.430 align:middle line:90%
Can I see what he's doing?

00:41:29.430 --> 00:41:31.420 align:middle line:84%
When he leaves, can I
pick it up from watching?

00:41:31.420 --> 00:41:32.295 align:middle line:90%
Totally miss that.

00:41:32.295 --> 00:41:33.670 align:middle line:84%
But then, what
about the midwife?

00:41:33.670 --> 00:41:36.180 align:middle line:90%
How is that different?

00:41:36.180 --> 00:41:38.167 align:middle line:84%
How do they describe the
midwife relationship?

00:41:38.167 --> 00:41:39.250 align:middle line:90%
Justin, I hope this is OK.

00:41:39.250 --> 00:41:39.820 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, sure.

00:41:39.820 --> 00:41:40.260 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I love this book.

00:41:40.260 --> 00:41:40.990 align:middle line:90%
So yeah.

00:41:40.990 --> 00:41:42.180 align:middle line:90%
Go ahead.

00:41:42.180 --> 00:41:44.640 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So I think one
of the main differences

00:41:44.640 --> 00:41:46.260 align:middle line:84%
is beyond the fact
that it wasn't

00:41:46.260 --> 00:41:47.800 align:middle line:90%
supposed to be more efficient.

00:41:47.800 --> 00:41:49.980 align:middle line:84%
So there was no
capitalist aspect to it.

00:41:49.980 --> 00:41:52.210 align:middle line:84%
There was a lot of
culture embedded in it.

00:41:52.210 --> 00:41:55.800 align:middle line:84%
So the daughter of a midwife
would become a midwife,

00:41:55.800 --> 00:41:57.300 align:middle line:84%
meaning that at
five years old, they

00:41:57.300 --> 00:42:00.210 align:middle line:84%
would already be present
in that situation

00:42:00.210 --> 00:42:03.990 align:middle line:84%
and start picking up
on what was going on,

00:42:03.990 --> 00:42:08.120 align:middle line:84%
and then progressively
have a more protagonist

00:42:08.120 --> 00:42:10.020 align:middle line:90%
role to that experience.

00:42:10.020 --> 00:42:10.520 align:middle line:90%
So.

00:42:10.520 --> 00:42:13.290 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, so although both
could be argued as peripheral--

00:42:13.290 --> 00:42:15.620 align:middle line:84%
we're in the same space,
we're hanging out--

00:42:15.620 --> 00:42:18.140 align:middle line:84%
who's welcome in the community,
and what type of knowledge

00:42:18.140 --> 00:42:20.270 align:middle line:84%
are you able to
gain from that type

00:42:20.270 --> 00:42:21.480 align:middle line:90%
of peripheral participation?

00:42:21.480 --> 00:42:22.230 align:middle line:90%
It's kind of cool.

00:42:22.230 --> 00:42:23.090 align:middle line:90%
It's subtle, right?

00:42:23.090 --> 00:42:23.460 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:42:23.460 --> 00:42:24.293 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: All right.

00:42:24.293 --> 00:42:25.220 align:middle line:90%
That's all I got.

00:42:25.220 --> 00:42:25.820 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Can I make--

00:42:25.820 --> 00:42:26.612 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:42:26.612 --> 00:42:27.830 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:42:27.830 --> 00:42:31.490 align:middle line:84%
The previous paragraph, I had
a lot of reflection about it.

00:42:31.490 --> 00:42:36.750 align:middle line:84%
And I don't-- that
in some aspects--

00:42:36.750 --> 00:42:38.280 align:middle line:84%
I'm not sure how
you feel about it,

00:42:38.280 --> 00:42:40.250 align:middle line:84%
but I really like
the point that he

00:42:40.250 --> 00:42:44.180 align:middle line:84%
makes about learning
kind of forms

00:42:44.180 --> 00:42:47.515 align:middle line:84%
your identity as part of
this social group, right?

00:42:47.515 --> 00:42:50.450 align:middle line:84%
And I think that really
applies to kids, for example.

00:42:50.450 --> 00:42:53.210 align:middle line:84%
They come from this
peripheral existence

00:42:53.210 --> 00:42:55.940 align:middle line:84%
and develop their own
identities and become

00:42:55.940 --> 00:42:59.400 align:middle line:84%
a committed, functioning
part of society.

00:42:59.400 --> 00:43:02.010 align:middle line:84%
But then it really
made me think, oh,

00:43:02.010 --> 00:43:05.660 align:middle line:84%
how do we think about it
from an ableist perspective,

00:43:05.660 --> 00:43:09.230 align:middle line:84%
where people cannot acquire
certain knowledges or certain

00:43:09.230 --> 00:43:12.510 align:middle line:84%
abilities, and does that mean
they have less of an identity?

00:43:12.510 --> 00:43:15.180 align:middle line:84%
I really thought about
that for a while.

00:43:15.180 --> 00:43:17.540 align:middle line:84%
And at the same time,
there is a criticism

00:43:17.540 --> 00:43:20.120 align:middle line:84%
in the book about
commoditization of knowledge

00:43:20.120 --> 00:43:22.830 align:middle line:84%
because before, with
midwives, et cetera,

00:43:22.830 --> 00:43:25.580 align:middle line:84%
it was this really specific
group of people teaching

00:43:25.580 --> 00:43:29.348 align:middle line:84%
this very kind of
expertise, whereas today, we

00:43:29.348 --> 00:43:30.890 align:middle line:84%
have universities
and we have schools

00:43:30.890 --> 00:43:34.200 align:middle line:84%
where you can get to whatever
class and learn things.

00:43:34.200 --> 00:43:36.840 align:middle line:84%
But it's not necessarily
all bad, right?

00:43:36.840 --> 00:43:38.915 align:middle line:84%
Because that would
imply that if you're

00:43:38.915 --> 00:43:40.790 align:middle line:84%
the daughter of the
midwife, you don't really

00:43:40.790 --> 00:43:44.630 align:middle line:84%
have a lot of say into
becoming or not a midwife.

00:43:44.630 --> 00:43:46.710 align:middle line:84%
The way that society was
structured back then,

00:43:46.710 --> 00:43:48.590 align:middle line:84%
that likely you would
kind of become one,

00:43:48.590 --> 00:43:50.130 align:middle line:84%
because that's what
expected of you.

00:43:50.130 --> 00:43:53.690 align:middle line:84%
Whereas when you
can choose that,

00:43:53.690 --> 00:43:56.430 align:middle line:84%
you have some sort
of agency and power,

00:43:56.430 --> 00:43:58.970 align:middle line:84%
even if you won't
have that exposure

00:43:58.970 --> 00:44:02.090 align:middle line:84%
as a peripheral participant
since you were a kid.

00:44:02.090 --> 00:44:04.367 align:middle line:84%
So it really made me
think of many things.

00:44:04.367 --> 00:44:05.200 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Great.

00:44:05.200 --> 00:44:05.877 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:44:05.877 --> 00:44:07.710 align:middle line:84%
And none of them have
to be resolved either.

00:44:07.710 --> 00:44:10.630 align:middle line:90%


00:44:10.630 --> 00:44:12.820 align:middle line:84%
Probably some important
things to take away

00:44:12.820 --> 00:44:15.952 align:middle line:84%
are like how Jean
Lave assigns values

00:44:15.952 --> 00:44:17.660 align:middle line:84%
to these different
kinds of institutions.

00:44:17.660 --> 00:44:19.430 align:middle line:90%
He thinks it's a big problem--

00:44:19.430 --> 00:44:21.280 align:middle line:84%
a big problem with
schools and university

00:44:21.280 --> 00:44:26.410 align:middle line:84%
is ripping knowledge away from
these broader social contexts,

00:44:26.410 --> 00:44:28.190 align:middle line:84%
that you go and take
a physics class,

00:44:28.190 --> 00:44:31.610 align:middle line:84%
and in the vast majority
of Physics 101 classes,

00:44:31.610 --> 00:44:33.773 align:middle line:84%
no one is trying to get
you to be a physicist.

00:44:33.773 --> 00:44:35.440 align:middle line:84%
They're not trying
to get you to develop

00:44:35.440 --> 00:44:36.625 align:middle line:90%
the identity of a physicist.

00:44:36.625 --> 00:44:38.500 align:middle line:84%
They're just trying to
teach you the physics.

00:44:38.500 --> 00:44:40.280 align:middle line:90%
And what is lost in that?

00:44:40.280 --> 00:44:42.130 align:middle line:84%
Of course, what's
pretty rad about walking

00:44:42.130 --> 00:44:44.480 align:middle line:84%
into a campus full of
people who'll be like, yeah,

00:44:44.480 --> 00:44:46.240 align:middle line:84%
I'll teach you any of these
atomized little things

00:44:46.240 --> 00:44:46.948 align:middle line:90%
for you to learn.

00:44:46.948 --> 00:44:49.090 align:middle line:90%
That seems kind of cool, too.

00:44:49.090 --> 00:44:51.640 align:middle line:84%
"It is worth noting that it
would be difficult to find

00:44:51.640 --> 00:44:53.980 align:middle line:84%
evidence that teaching
is the mode of knowledge

00:44:53.980 --> 00:44:56.710 align:middle line:84%
'transmission'
amongst the midwives."

00:44:56.710 --> 00:44:59.730 align:middle line:84%
He makes his claim that you
wouldn't find direct instruction

00:44:59.730 --> 00:45:01.210 align:middle line:90%
amongst the midwives.

00:45:01.210 --> 00:45:03.220 align:middle line:84%
I mean, Jordan did the
fieldwork and not me.

00:45:03.220 --> 00:45:05.490 align:middle line:84%
That seems totally
laughable to me.

00:45:05.490 --> 00:45:08.040 align:middle line:84%
I've spent time with lots of
people of lots of ages, learned

00:45:08.040 --> 00:45:10.830 align:middle line:84%
lots of kinds of things, and
people are always transmitting

00:45:10.830 --> 00:45:11.950 align:middle line:90%
and explaining stuff.

00:45:11.950 --> 00:45:13.860 align:middle line:90%
It's always nested in there.

00:45:13.860 --> 00:45:15.908 align:middle line:84%
But there's a kind of
politics of Jean Lave,

00:45:15.908 --> 00:45:17.950 align:middle line:84%
like, we don't even need
to explain these things.

00:45:17.950 --> 00:45:19.480 align:middle line:84%
We don't even need to
teach these people.

00:45:19.480 --> 00:45:21.230 align:middle line:84%
We can learn it just
like babies learn it,

00:45:21.230 --> 00:45:22.710 align:middle line:90%
in their natural environment.

00:45:22.710 --> 00:45:24.990 align:middle line:84%
Just like you have to
surround babies with language

00:45:24.990 --> 00:45:27.420 align:middle line:84%
and they'll learn language,
you surround young girls

00:45:27.420 --> 00:45:30.430 align:middle line:84%
with birth and pregnancy
and they learn midwifery.

00:45:30.430 --> 00:45:32.803 align:middle line:90%


00:45:32.803 --> 00:45:35.220 align:middle line:84%
"Apprenticeship happens as a
way of, and in the course of,

00:45:35.220 --> 00:45:35.920 align:middle line:90%
daily life.

00:45:35.920 --> 00:45:38.170 align:middle line:84%
It may not be recognized as
a teaching effort at all."

00:45:38.170 --> 00:45:39.378 align:middle line:90%
Again, this is doubling down.

00:45:39.378 --> 00:45:41.020 align:middle line:90%
There's no instruction here.

00:45:41.020 --> 00:45:43.560 align:middle line:84%
If there's instruction, the
people are lying to you,

00:45:43.560 --> 00:45:45.300 align:middle line:90%
and you should despise them.

00:45:45.300 --> 00:45:47.850 align:middle line:84%
"A Maya girl who eventually
became a midwife, most likely

00:45:47.850 --> 00:45:49.930 align:middle line:84%
has a mother or a
grandmother as a midwife.

00:45:49.930 --> 00:45:52.110 align:middle line:84%
Girls in such families,
without being identified

00:45:52.110 --> 00:45:54.570 align:middle line:84%
as apprentice midwives,
absorb the essence

00:45:54.570 --> 00:45:56.820 align:middle line:84%
of midwifery practice as
well as specific knowledge

00:45:56.820 --> 00:45:59.680 align:middle line:84%
about many procedures, simply
in the process of growing up.

00:45:59.680 --> 00:46:01.180 align:middle line:84%
They know what the
life of a midwife

00:46:01.180 --> 00:46:03.430 align:middle line:84%
is like, what kinds of
stories the women and men who

00:46:03.430 --> 00:46:06.100 align:middle line:84%
come to consult her tell, what
kind of herbs and other remedies

00:46:06.100 --> 00:46:07.330 align:middle line:90%
might be needed to collected.

00:46:07.330 --> 00:46:10.270 align:middle line:84%
As young children, they might be
sitting quietly in their corner

00:46:10.270 --> 00:46:12.918 align:middle line:84%
as their mother administers
a prenatal massage.

00:46:12.918 --> 00:46:14.710 align:middle line:84%
They would hear stories
of difficult cases,

00:46:14.710 --> 00:46:16.252 align:middle line:84%
of miraculous
outcomes, and the like.

00:46:16.252 --> 00:46:19.010 align:middle line:84%
As they grow older, they
may be passing messages,

00:46:19.010 --> 00:46:21.140 align:middle line:84%
running errands,
getting needed supplies.

00:46:21.140 --> 00:46:23.440 align:middle line:84%
A young girl might be
present as her mother stops

00:46:23.440 --> 00:46:25.600 align:middle line:84%
for a postpartum visit
after the daily shopping

00:46:25.600 --> 00:46:26.630 align:middle line:90%
trip to the market.

00:46:26.630 --> 00:46:28.520 align:middle line:84%
Eventually, after she
has a child herself,

00:46:28.520 --> 00:46:29.937 align:middle line:84%
she might come
along to her birth,

00:46:29.937 --> 00:46:32.140 align:middle line:84%
perhaps because her ailing
grandmother needs someone

00:46:32.140 --> 00:46:33.520 align:middle line:84%
to walk with, and
thus finds herself

00:46:33.520 --> 00:46:35.800 align:middle line:84%
doing for the woman in labor
what other women had done

00:46:35.800 --> 00:46:37.130 align:middle line:90%
for her when she gave birth.

00:46:37.130 --> 00:46:39.980 align:middle line:84%
That is, she may take a turn at
supporting the laboring woman.

00:46:39.980 --> 00:46:42.850 align:middle line:84%
Eventually, she may even
administer prenatal massages

00:46:42.850 --> 00:46:44.040 align:middle line:90%
to the selected clients.

00:46:44.040 --> 00:46:45.790 align:middle line:84%
At some point, she may
decide she actually

00:46:45.790 --> 00:46:47.040 align:middle line:90%
wants to do this kind of work.

00:46:47.040 --> 00:46:49.180 align:middle line:84%
She then pays more
attention, but only rarely

00:46:49.180 --> 00:46:50.420 align:middle line:90%
does she ask questions.

00:46:50.420 --> 00:46:52.130 align:middle line:84%
Her mentor sees
their association,

00:46:52.130 --> 00:46:54.590 align:middle line:84%
primarily one that is
of some use to her.

00:46:54.590 --> 00:46:56.270 align:middle line:84%
'Rosa already knows
how to do a massage,

00:46:56.270 --> 00:46:58.400 align:middle line:84%
so I can send her
if I'm too busy.'"

00:46:58.400 --> 00:47:00.260 align:middle line:84%
That is just a
beautiful description

00:47:00.260 --> 00:47:02.743 align:middle line:84%
of legitimate
peripheral participation

00:47:02.743 --> 00:47:04.410 align:middle line:84%
moving from the
periphery to the center.

00:47:04.410 --> 00:47:06.120 align:middle line:84%
You just watch this
thing for a while.

00:47:06.120 --> 00:47:08.340 align:middle line:84%
And then your mom's
hands are a little sore,

00:47:08.340 --> 00:47:10.003 align:middle line:84%
and so you do the
massage this time.

00:47:10.003 --> 00:47:11.420 align:middle line:84%
And you know how
to do the massage

00:47:11.420 --> 00:47:12.780 align:middle line:90%
because it happened to you.

00:47:12.780 --> 00:47:14.280 align:middle line:90%
You've seen it a zillion times.

00:47:14.280 --> 00:47:16.640 align:middle line:84%
Are any of you part
of clubs or groups

00:47:16.640 --> 00:47:19.270 align:middle line:84%
at MIT that have learning
that feels like this?

00:47:19.270 --> 00:47:23.450 align:middle line:90%


00:47:23.450 --> 00:47:25.700 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I guess, technically,
a lot of theater clubs

00:47:25.700 --> 00:47:26.388 align:middle line:90%
are similar.

00:47:26.388 --> 00:47:27.180 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:47:27.180 --> 00:47:28.310 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: People
are just doing this.

00:47:28.310 --> 00:47:29.670 align:middle line:84%
How do you become
a stage manager?

00:47:29.670 --> 00:47:31.700 align:middle line:84%
I don't know, look at what
the stage manager does.

00:47:31.700 --> 00:47:32.510 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: And
the other thing

00:47:32.510 --> 00:47:34.370 align:middle line:84%
is like, you start
as a stagehand.

00:47:34.370 --> 00:47:35.240 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:47:35.240 --> 00:47:36.480 align:middle line:90%
You watch some theater.

00:47:36.480 --> 00:47:37.610 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, just stand there.

00:47:37.610 --> 00:47:40.910 align:middle line:84%
And between the second
and third act, hand

00:47:40.910 --> 00:47:42.920 align:middle line:90%
this prop to that person.

00:47:42.920 --> 00:47:45.420 align:middle line:84%
And you're probably never going
to take stage manager class.

00:47:45.420 --> 00:47:46.920 align:middle line:84%
You're just going to
hand that prop once,

00:47:46.920 --> 00:47:48.420 align:middle line:90%
and then you're the prop master.

00:47:48.420 --> 00:47:49.700 align:middle line:84%
And then the stage
manager gets sick,

00:47:49.700 --> 00:47:51.780 align:middle line:84%
and you do it for the night, and
then you're the stage manager.

00:47:51.780 --> 00:47:53.905 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, I think theater has
a ton of stuff like this.

00:47:53.905 --> 00:47:54.940 align:middle line:90%
That's a great example.

00:47:54.940 --> 00:47:55.990 align:middle line:84%
What were you
going to say, Omar?

00:47:55.990 --> 00:47:57.490 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: In The
Tech and Technique,

00:47:57.490 --> 00:47:59.093 align:middle line:90%
we have what's called shadowing.

00:47:59.093 --> 00:48:00.760 align:middle line:84%
So when we go out for
photo assignments,

00:48:00.760 --> 00:48:02.635 align:middle line:84%
where there's a studio
photography assignment

00:48:02.635 --> 00:48:04.780 align:middle line:84%
or something out
in the field, we

00:48:04.780 --> 00:48:06.525 align:middle line:84%
can always have
someone shadowing us,

00:48:06.525 --> 00:48:08.900 align:middle line:84%
and they can also be taking
photos with their own camera.

00:48:08.900 --> 00:48:11.180 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes, we will
use their work as well.

00:48:11.180 --> 00:48:13.390 align:middle line:84%
So they can also do
legitimate periphery work.

00:48:13.390 --> 00:48:14.390 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: I love it.

00:48:14.390 --> 00:48:17.480 align:middle line:84%
And you have intentionally
held space for that.

00:48:17.480 --> 00:48:20.740 align:middle line:84%
You've kind of designed like,
oh, yeah, come along, hang out

00:48:20.740 --> 00:48:21.295 align:middle line:90%
with us.

00:48:21.295 --> 00:48:23.170 align:middle line:84%
And you probably have
parties and other kinds

00:48:23.170 --> 00:48:24.712 align:middle line:84%
of things like that
where it's like--

00:48:24.712 --> 00:48:27.170 align:middle line:84%
where some of it you're just
goofing around, but some of it

00:48:27.170 --> 00:48:29.650 align:middle line:84%
you're like, oh, the story that
we got, da, da, da, da, da.

00:48:29.650 --> 00:48:32.860 align:middle line:84%
Them telling the stories of
difficult cases and miraculous

00:48:32.860 --> 00:48:33.500 align:middle line:90%
outcomes.

00:48:33.500 --> 00:48:36.460 align:middle line:84%
I'm sure the tech has other
similar kinds of difficult cases

00:48:36.460 --> 00:48:37.790 align:middle line:90%
and miraculous outcomes.

00:48:37.790 --> 00:48:39.760 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was going
to say, on sports teams,

00:48:39.760 --> 00:48:42.730 align:middle line:84%
when people become captains, not
often is the coach sitting them

00:48:42.730 --> 00:48:45.410 align:middle line:84%
down and being like, this
is how you be a captain,

00:48:45.410 --> 00:48:46.790 align:middle line:90%
this is how you lead people.

00:48:46.790 --> 00:48:48.640 align:middle line:84%
It's more like
they just naturally

00:48:48.640 --> 00:48:52.300 align:middle line:84%
have this personality of doing
that, of maybe they grew up

00:48:52.300 --> 00:48:55.560 align:middle line:84%
and their mom was a
girlboss, or they just

00:48:55.560 --> 00:48:57.190 align:middle line:90%
grew up seeing stuff like that.

00:48:57.190 --> 00:49:00.180 align:middle line:84%
So then you have that
natural progression

00:49:00.180 --> 00:49:01.798 align:middle line:90%
into just learning by doing.

00:49:01.798 --> 00:49:02.590 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:49:02.590 --> 00:49:03.210 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yeah.

00:49:03.210 --> 00:49:04.710 align:middle line:84%
What were you going
to say, Sabrina?

00:49:04.710 --> 00:49:06.780 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: OK, this is
kind of a dumb example--

00:49:06.780 --> 00:49:07.710 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: I like them.

00:49:07.710 --> 00:49:09.127 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I'm
really bad at Smash,

00:49:09.127 --> 00:49:11.680 align:middle line:84%
so I always get placed
with the best Smash player.

00:49:11.680 --> 00:49:14.250 align:middle line:84%
And then we're on a team
against two other people who

00:49:14.250 --> 00:49:15.630 align:middle line:90%
are also really good.

00:49:15.630 --> 00:49:17.208 align:middle line:84%
But this person
is so good that--

00:49:17.208 --> 00:49:18.250 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: They carry.

00:49:18.250 --> 00:49:19.125 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: They carry.

00:49:19.125 --> 00:49:22.230 align:middle line:84%
But I'm kind of learning
along with them.

00:49:22.230 --> 00:49:26.070 align:middle line:84%
And I kind of hold off onto the
side, where I just kind of try

00:49:26.070 --> 00:49:30.000 align:middle line:84%
to hold lives while they
destroy the other people.

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:31.720 align:middle line:84%
And I think that's
kind of periphery.

00:49:31.720 --> 00:49:33.428 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Oh,
yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:49:33.428 --> 00:49:35.740 align:middle line:84%
I mean, in that
particular community,

00:49:35.740 --> 00:49:38.490 align:middle line:84%
it's a space of legitimate
peripheral participation, which

00:49:38.490 --> 00:49:41.910 align:middle line:84%
somewhat exists because of
this fairness, balancing

00:49:41.910 --> 00:49:42.970 align:middle line:90%
kinds of things.

00:49:42.970 --> 00:49:45.600 align:middle line:84%
And so every
learning environment,

00:49:45.600 --> 00:49:48.450 align:middle line:84%
the substance of
Super Smash Brothers

00:49:48.450 --> 00:49:52.110 align:middle line:84%
determines where the
legitimate-- and it determines

00:49:52.110 --> 00:49:53.580 align:middle line:84%
spaces for peripheral
participation

00:49:53.580 --> 00:49:55.920 align:middle line:84%
that's different than
journalism, different

00:49:55.920 --> 00:49:56.490 align:middle line:90%
than sports.

00:49:56.490 --> 00:49:57.240 align:middle line:90%
They're different.

00:49:57.240 --> 00:49:59.250 align:middle line:84%
But this theory is
giving us commonality,

00:49:59.250 --> 00:50:00.930 align:middle line:84%
where we can talk about
really different things that

00:50:00.930 --> 00:50:02.350 align:middle line:84%
might arise in
different circumstances.

00:50:02.350 --> 00:50:04.630 align:middle line:84%
But I think that's actually
kind of the same thing.

00:50:04.630 --> 00:50:09.000 align:middle line:84%
And there's kind of spaces
that I'm finding for learning.

00:50:09.000 --> 00:50:11.640 align:middle line:84%
"In short, when official
channels offer only

00:50:11.640 --> 00:50:14.910 align:middle line:84%
a possibility to participate in
institutionally mandated forms

00:50:14.910 --> 00:50:17.315 align:middle line:84%
of commoditized activity,
genuine participation,

00:50:17.315 --> 00:50:18.690 align:middle line:84%
membership, and
legitimate access

00:50:18.690 --> 00:50:21.360 align:middle line:84%
to ongoing practice-- of a
practice considered worthy

00:50:21.360 --> 00:50:22.540 align:middle line:90%
of the name-- are rare.

00:50:22.540 --> 00:50:25.025 align:middle line:84%
Practice is not to be found
in schools and workplaces.

00:50:25.025 --> 00:50:27.150 align:middle line:84%
At the same time, schools
and school-like workplace

00:50:27.150 --> 00:50:29.720 align:middle line:84%
educational enterprises accord
knowledge skill a reified

00:50:29.720 --> 00:50:31.470 align:middle line:84%
existence, turning it
into something to be

00:50:31.470 --> 00:50:35.050 align:middle line:84%
'acquired' and its transmission
into an institutional motive.

00:50:35.050 --> 00:50:37.770 align:middle line:84%
This process generates pressures
towards the trivializing

00:50:37.770 --> 00:50:39.670 align:middle line:84%
decomposing of
forms or activity.

00:50:39.670 --> 00:50:42.120 align:middle line:84%
The result is a widespread
generation of negative

00:50:42.120 --> 00:50:45.750 align:middle line:84%
identities, of misrecognized
or institutionally disapproved

00:50:45.750 --> 00:50:48.540 align:middle line:84%
interstitial communities
of practice."

00:50:48.540 --> 00:50:50.660 align:middle line:84%
Some of the things that
might feel most academic

00:50:50.660 --> 00:50:54.950 align:middle line:84%
might feel least
identity-oriented to you

00:50:54.950 --> 00:50:58.292 align:middle line:84%
because there's not-- think
about, what degrees here would

00:50:58.292 --> 00:50:59.750 align:middle line:84%
be like, I'm not
sure there's a way

00:50:59.750 --> 00:51:02.210 align:middle line:84%
to be a legitimate
peripheral participant there?

00:51:02.210 --> 00:51:04.500 align:middle line:84%
There's a way to
learn the stuff.

00:51:04.500 --> 00:51:07.220 align:middle line:84%
There's a way to be taught the
discrete parts of this course

00:51:07.220 --> 00:51:08.160 align:middle line:90%
or subject or major.

00:51:08.160 --> 00:51:11.820 align:middle line:84%
But I'm not learning how to
be a part of this community.

00:51:11.820 --> 00:51:14.900 align:middle line:90%


00:51:14.900 --> 00:51:17.180 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:51:17.180 --> 00:51:21.110 align:middle line:84%
Here's three things that
I'll have you do together

00:51:21.110 --> 00:51:24.955 align:middle line:84%
for 5 or 10 minutes, which
might involve some drawing.

00:51:24.955 --> 00:51:27.560 align:middle line:90%


00:51:27.560 --> 00:51:31.100 align:middle line:84%
So grab some paper
or some board space.

00:51:31.100 --> 00:51:34.160 align:middle line:84%
See if you can draw the
equivalent of human cognitive

00:51:34.160 --> 00:51:35.960 align:middle line:90%
architecture.

00:51:35.960 --> 00:51:37.460 align:middle line:90%
See if you can draw--

00:51:37.460 --> 00:51:42.338 align:middle line:84%
just like you drew these
diagrams for cognitive load

00:51:42.338 --> 00:51:43.880 align:middle line:84%
theory, what is the
diagram you would

00:51:43.880 --> 00:51:49.250 align:middle line:90%
draw for situated learning?

00:51:49.250 --> 00:51:52.850 align:middle line:84%
Based on that
cognitive architecture,

00:51:52.850 --> 00:51:54.750 align:middle line:84%
what is the pedagogy
that follows?

00:51:54.750 --> 00:51:58.590 align:middle line:84%
So based on the key fundamental
principles of situated learning,

00:51:58.590 --> 00:52:00.200 align:middle line:84%
cognitive load
theory said, here's

00:52:00.200 --> 00:52:01.920 align:middle line:84%
a model of human
cognitive architecture.

00:52:01.920 --> 00:52:03.950 align:middle line:84%
Here are a series of
clearly defined effects.

00:52:03.950 --> 00:52:05.450 align:middle line:84%
The situated learning
people are not

00:52:05.450 --> 00:52:08.880 align:middle line:84%
as good as enumerating in bullet
points all of their pieces,

00:52:08.880 --> 00:52:10.250 align:middle line:90%
but it's all in there.

00:52:10.250 --> 00:52:13.400 align:middle line:84%
What are some of the
instructional practices

00:52:13.400 --> 00:52:14.787 align:middle line:90%
that follow from this model?

00:52:14.787 --> 00:52:16.370 align:middle line:84%
Think about those
two things, and then

00:52:16.370 --> 00:52:20.780 align:middle line:84%
also see if you can recreate
some of your Venn diagrams of,

00:52:20.780 --> 00:52:22.460 align:middle line:84%
what are the categories
of things that

00:52:22.460 --> 00:52:25.800 align:middle line:84%
seem separate from cognitive
learning and situated learning,

00:52:25.800 --> 00:52:28.580 align:middle line:84%
and what are the things that
they seem to be quite similar on

00:52:28.580 --> 00:52:29.970 align:middle line:90%
or quite together on?

00:52:29.970 --> 00:52:33.210 align:middle line:84%
Take 10 minutes to, again, grab
one person or two other people,

00:52:33.210 --> 00:52:34.830 align:middle line:84%
do some drawing,
do some composing.

00:52:34.830 --> 00:52:36.380 align:middle line:84%
Feel free to pull up your
notes from your learning

00:52:36.380 --> 00:52:38.782 align:middle line:84%
journal, where you should have
done some of these things.

00:52:38.782 --> 00:52:40.240 align:middle line:84%
And if any of these
questions-- you

00:52:40.240 --> 00:52:42.600 align:middle line:84%
don't have to spend equal
time on all three questions.

00:52:42.600 --> 00:52:43.770 align:middle line:84%
If you dive in on
one, that's fine.

00:52:43.770 --> 00:52:46.310 align:middle line:84%
But those three should keep you
busy for 8 minutes or something

00:52:46.310 --> 00:52:46.810 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:52:46.810 --> 00:52:47.400 align:middle line:90%
Make sense?

00:52:47.400 --> 00:52:47.900 align:middle line:90%
Ready?

00:52:47.900 --> 00:52:50.420 align:middle line:90%
Go.

00:52:50.420 --> 00:52:53.030 align:middle line:84%
So one thing I love about
almost all of these drawings

00:52:53.030 --> 00:52:55.460 align:middle line:84%
is it's kind of impossible
to draw these models

00:52:55.460 --> 00:52:58.940 align:middle line:84%
without drawing other people,
that when you all developed

00:52:58.940 --> 00:53:01.040 align:middle line:84%
a kind of human
cognitive architecture

00:53:01.040 --> 00:53:04.490 align:middle line:84%
in cognitive load theory,
you're drawing one brain.

00:53:04.490 --> 00:53:06.648 align:middle line:84%
And actually, most
of your drawings,

00:53:06.648 --> 00:53:08.940 align:middle line:84%
you didn't even really
represent the rest of the world.

00:53:08.940 --> 00:53:10.430 align:middle line:84%
But in this model,
it's impossible

00:53:10.430 --> 00:53:12.055 align:middle line:84%
not to represent the
rest of the world,

00:53:12.055 --> 00:53:15.170 align:middle line:84%
and in particular, other people,
because learning fundamentally

00:53:15.170 --> 00:53:17.150 align:middle line:84%
is social, and your
identity is fundamentally

00:53:17.150 --> 00:53:20.490 align:middle line:84%
reflective of your
connections with other people.

00:53:20.490 --> 00:53:23.090 align:middle line:90%
So these are good.

00:53:23.090 --> 00:53:25.950 align:middle line:84%
You read a little piece
of "Give P's a Chance."

00:53:25.950 --> 00:53:29.900 align:middle line:84%
So four P's from Mitch Resnick
about Projects, Passion, Play,

00:53:29.900 --> 00:53:30.630 align:middle line:90%
and Peers.

00:53:30.630 --> 00:53:33.830 align:middle line:84%
Hopefully you can now start
mapping some of these ideas

00:53:33.830 --> 00:53:35.630 align:middle line:90%
to what we talked about--

00:53:35.630 --> 00:53:38.750 align:middle line:84%
projects, open-ended
explorations,

00:53:38.750 --> 00:53:42.240 align:middle line:84%
making music,
making funny shape,

00:53:42.240 --> 00:53:44.840 align:middle line:84%
having a robot draw
funny shapes somewhere.

00:53:44.840 --> 00:53:47.770 align:middle line:84%
Those are spaces for
open-ended exploration,

00:53:47.770 --> 00:53:49.830 align:middle line:84%
for doing multiple
kinds of things.

00:53:49.830 --> 00:53:53.260 align:middle line:84%
Projects have more space for
peripheral participation,

00:53:53.260 --> 00:53:55.860 align:middle line:84%
like multiple people can be
making music even if someone's

00:53:55.860 --> 00:53:59.250 align:middle line:84%
a little bit better at making
music than the other person,

00:53:59.250 --> 00:54:06.180 align:middle line:84%
that learning environments
here are driven by interests.

00:54:06.180 --> 00:54:09.240 align:middle line:84%
There's very little reference
in cognitive load theory

00:54:09.240 --> 00:54:11.730 align:middle line:84%
to things like, well, yeah,
sometimes the kids don't

00:54:11.730 --> 00:54:14.430 align:middle line:84%
like learning math, so they
just don't, although that's

00:54:14.430 --> 00:54:15.870 align:middle line:90%
very real.

00:54:15.870 --> 00:54:17.845 align:middle line:90%
Some of the young--

00:54:17.845 --> 00:54:20.220 align:middle line:84%
some of the daughters of
midwives want to become midwives

00:54:20.220 --> 00:54:21.780 align:middle line:90%
and some didn't.

00:54:21.780 --> 00:54:24.700 align:middle line:84%
And for the ones who did
want to become midwives,

00:54:24.700 --> 00:54:25.870 align:middle line:90%
they followed that interest.

00:54:25.870 --> 00:54:28.212 align:middle line:84%
And those who didn't went
and did other things.

00:54:28.212 --> 00:54:29.920 align:middle line:84%
And in these kinds of
models of learning,

00:54:29.920 --> 00:54:31.805 align:middle line:84%
that's actually OK
and really important.

00:54:31.805 --> 00:54:34.180 align:middle line:84%
We're not supposed to make
everyone better at everything.

00:54:34.180 --> 00:54:36.030 align:middle line:84%
We're supposed to let people
follow their interests

00:54:36.030 --> 00:54:38.610 align:middle line:84%
and get good at the things
that they want to get good at,

00:54:38.610 --> 00:54:42.750 align:middle line:84%
that in environments in which
there is a kind of lightness

00:54:42.750 --> 00:54:46.940 align:middle line:84%
to the environment,
lightness and play create

00:54:46.940 --> 00:54:50.300 align:middle line:84%
space for multiple
forms of participation.

00:54:50.300 --> 00:54:56.340 align:middle line:84%
Things don't have to be
optimized to get to the fastest,

00:54:56.340 --> 00:54:57.780 align:middle line:90%
best trajectory for all people.

00:54:57.780 --> 00:55:01.190 align:middle line:84%
There can be room to
play and mess around

00:55:01.190 --> 00:55:02.370 align:middle line:90%
and try other things.

00:55:02.370 --> 00:55:04.010 align:middle line:84%
And then doing it
with other people

00:55:04.010 --> 00:55:07.100 align:middle line:84%
is essential, as you can see in
all of these kinds of things.

00:55:07.100 --> 00:55:10.830 align:middle line:84%
And part of what, in some
ways, the real genius--

00:55:10.830 --> 00:55:11.970 align:middle line:90%
Jean Lave was a woman.

00:55:11.970 --> 00:55:13.920 align:middle line:84%
I misrepresented
her as a man before,

00:55:13.920 --> 00:55:15.650 align:middle line:90%
and Erin helpfully corrected me.

00:55:15.650 --> 00:55:21.420 align:middle line:84%
Etienne Wenger, her partner,
is a co-writer, is a guy.

00:55:21.420 --> 00:55:24.330 align:middle line:84%
It's not just people
hanging out with each other.

00:55:24.330 --> 00:55:27.860 align:middle line:84%
We can describe
repeated patterns

00:55:27.860 --> 00:55:32.150 align:middle line:84%
we see of legitimate peripheral
participation of old timers

00:55:32.150 --> 00:55:34.500 align:middle line:90%
and newcomers.

00:55:34.500 --> 00:55:36.530 align:middle line:84%
There's lots in Lave's
work that you probably

00:55:36.530 --> 00:55:39.440 align:middle line:84%
read that was about trying to
find new language to get away

00:55:39.440 --> 00:55:42.390 align:middle line:84%
from the experts and novices,
because that's part of it,

00:55:42.390 --> 00:55:43.760 align:middle line:90%
but it's not just that.

00:55:43.760 --> 00:55:45.260 align:middle line:84%
It's our time in
a community, it's

00:55:45.260 --> 00:55:47.400 align:middle line:84%
our space in the community,
those kinds of things.

00:55:47.400 --> 00:55:50.820 align:middle line:84%
So just like in cognitive
load theory you could say,

00:55:50.820 --> 00:55:52.467 align:middle line:84%
here's a human
cognitive architecture,

00:55:52.467 --> 00:55:54.050 align:middle line:84%
here's a set of
pedagogical principles

00:55:54.050 --> 00:55:56.870 align:middle line:84%
that follow from that,
Jean Lave can describe

00:55:56.870 --> 00:55:58.140 align:middle line:90%
these social interactions.

00:55:58.140 --> 00:56:03.418 align:middle line:84%
You can abstract from them
some models of human cognitive

00:56:03.418 --> 00:56:04.710 align:middle line:90%
architecture and say, well, OK.

00:56:04.710 --> 00:56:07.430 align:middle line:84%
From those models, there are
some instructional principles

00:56:07.430 --> 00:56:08.750 align:middle line:90%
that follow.

00:56:08.750 --> 00:56:10.898 align:middle line:84%
If I were to draw one of
these, I think a thing

00:56:10.898 --> 00:56:12.440 align:middle line:84%
that I would pay a
bunch of attention

00:56:12.440 --> 00:56:17.600 align:middle line:84%
to is that the thing in
both learning models,

00:56:17.600 --> 00:56:19.430 align:middle line:90%
you learn what you attend to.

00:56:19.430 --> 00:56:21.680 align:middle line:90%
Seeing is really important.

00:56:21.680 --> 00:56:25.880 align:middle line:84%
A key insight of
situated learning

00:56:25.880 --> 00:56:29.460 align:middle line:84%
is that we attend to things
that interest us and motivate us

00:56:29.460 --> 00:56:32.240 align:middle line:84%
and where we find our
identity and care about.

00:56:32.240 --> 00:56:35.037 align:middle line:84%
If looking at something
is essential to get people

00:56:35.037 --> 00:56:37.370 align:middle line:84%
to learn about it, then we
ought to think very carefully

00:56:37.370 --> 00:56:41.300 align:middle line:84%
about what motivates
us to look at a thing.

00:56:41.300 --> 00:56:50.140 align:middle line:84%
T-ball, for me, is a canonical
example of a kind of activity

00:56:50.140 --> 00:56:53.350 align:middle line:84%
which would be almost
impossible to teach primarily

00:56:53.350 --> 00:56:55.510 align:middle line:90%
through cognitive load theory.

00:56:55.510 --> 00:56:57.918 align:middle line:84%
It's really hard to imagine
teaching kids baseball

00:56:57.918 --> 00:56:59.960 align:middle line:84%
where you're like, all
right, sit down, everyone.

00:56:59.960 --> 00:57:03.080 align:middle line:84%
We're all going to watch
a worked example of how

00:57:03.080 --> 00:57:05.208 align:middle line:90%
you hit a ball.

00:57:05.208 --> 00:57:07.000 align:middle line:84%
We have to learn the
rule book of baseball.

00:57:07.000 --> 00:57:09.400 align:middle line:84%
The way we teach kids
baseball is we're

00:57:09.400 --> 00:57:11.530 align:middle line:84%
like, what is the
fastest way we can

00:57:11.530 --> 00:57:14.140 align:middle line:84%
get young people, with limited
coordination and no knowledge

00:57:14.140 --> 00:57:16.610 align:middle line:84%
of the rules, to play
something like baseball?

00:57:16.610 --> 00:57:19.040 align:middle line:84%
How do we get them baseballing
as soon as we possibly can?

00:57:19.040 --> 00:57:21.100 align:middle line:84%
And some genius was like,
don't do the pitching.

00:57:21.100 --> 00:57:22.100 align:middle line:90%
Get rid of the pitching.

00:57:22.100 --> 00:57:25.420 align:middle line:84%
Just put the ball on the stick,
and they can just hit the ball.

00:57:25.420 --> 00:57:29.050 align:middle line:84%
It has some kind of
cognitive, some relationship

00:57:29.050 --> 00:57:33.060 align:middle line:84%
to part-whole practice, to
devolving scaffolding and things

00:57:33.060 --> 00:57:33.560 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:57:33.560 --> 00:57:36.010 align:middle line:84%
There's a researcher
at Harvard who

00:57:36.010 --> 00:57:38.693 align:middle line:84%
wrote a book called Making
Learning Whole, where he said

00:57:38.693 --> 00:57:40.360 align:middle line:84%
that one of the things
that we can often

00:57:40.360 --> 00:57:42.300 align:middle line:84%
do in curriculum
that we underestimate

00:57:42.300 --> 00:57:46.665 align:middle line:84%
is get people playing the whole
game as early as possible.

00:57:46.665 --> 00:57:47.790 align:middle line:90%
We'll talk about this more.

00:57:47.790 --> 00:57:49.470 align:middle line:84%
But think about,
what subjects have

00:57:49.470 --> 00:57:52.590 align:middle line:84%
you taken at MIT that let you
start playing the whole game as

00:57:52.590 --> 00:57:53.680 align:middle line:90%
soon as possible?

00:57:53.680 --> 00:57:57.930 align:middle line:84%
And what require you to build
up lots of elementary pieces

00:57:57.930 --> 00:58:01.830 align:middle line:84%
before you can do the thing
that whole discipline does?

00:58:01.830 --> 00:58:04.750 align:middle line:84%
But baseball instruction
in the United States,

00:58:04.750 --> 00:58:07.800 align:middle line:84%
very difficult to imagine
in a cognitive load theory

00:58:07.800 --> 00:58:10.830 align:middle line:84%
framework, very
pleasant for millions

00:58:10.830 --> 00:58:13.080 align:middle line:84%
of young people in
a more experiential,

00:58:13.080 --> 00:58:14.390 align:middle line:90%
situated kind of way.

00:58:14.390 --> 00:58:14.890 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:58:14.890 --> 00:58:17.440 align:middle line:84%
You did this a little
bit, which is good.

00:58:17.440 --> 00:58:19.260 align:middle line:84%
Here is one thing
that I would like

00:58:19.260 --> 00:58:23.070 align:middle line:84%
to have in the center of
your Venn diagrams of things

00:58:23.070 --> 00:58:24.570 align:middle line:90%
that are similar.

00:58:24.570 --> 00:58:26.700 align:middle line:84%
This is a misconception
that my students

00:58:26.700 --> 00:58:28.658 align:middle line:84%
have had over the years
that I want to correct.

00:58:28.658 --> 00:58:31.620 align:middle line:90%


00:58:31.620 --> 00:58:34.710 align:middle line:84%
The cognitive load theorists
and the situated learning people

00:58:34.710 --> 00:58:37.330 align:middle line:84%
agree on what
expertise looks like.

00:58:37.330 --> 00:58:39.675 align:middle line:84%
They agree on what high-quality
practice looks like.

00:58:39.675 --> 00:58:42.300 align:middle line:84%
I don't think you'd find these
people-- they would not disagree

00:58:42.300 --> 00:58:44.890 align:middle line:84%
about what a great baseball
player, or a great carpenter,

00:58:44.890 --> 00:58:47.790 align:middle line:84%
or a great surgeon, or a
great lawyer looks like.

00:58:47.790 --> 00:58:50.160 align:middle line:84%
They would believe
that-- sometimes people

00:58:50.160 --> 00:58:52.380 align:middle line:84%
think that the cognitive
load theorists want

00:58:52.380 --> 00:58:57.300 align:middle line:84%
people who are experts to be
really rigid and disciplined

00:58:57.300 --> 00:58:59.920 align:middle line:84%
and pattern repeating,
and that is not the case.

00:58:59.920 --> 00:59:01.870 align:middle line:84%
That is not what they
want as the endpoint.

00:59:01.870 --> 00:59:06.480 align:middle line:84%
They want your cancer surgeon
to be dynamic and flexible when

00:59:06.480 --> 00:59:09.420 align:middle line:84%
they cut open your belly
and find cancerous tumors

00:59:09.420 --> 00:59:10.890 align:middle line:84%
and are pulling
them out of there.

00:59:10.890 --> 00:59:13.300 align:middle line:84%
They want poets to be
creative and imaginative.

00:59:13.300 --> 00:59:16.800 align:middle line:84%
They just think that the way
that you develop the knowledge

00:59:16.800 --> 00:59:20.880 align:middle line:84%
base that lets you be creative
and flexible and dynamic and

00:59:20.880 --> 00:59:23.895 align:middle line:84%
strategic is through really
disciplined instruction.

00:59:23.895 --> 00:59:26.520 align:middle line:84%
Because if you don't go through
a process of really disciplined

00:59:26.520 --> 00:59:29.040 align:middle line:84%
instruction, it's hard
to get good at things

00:59:29.040 --> 00:59:30.960 align:middle line:84%
because no one
has showed you how

00:59:30.960 --> 00:59:34.140 align:middle line:84%
to do fundamental
pieces in clear ways.

00:59:34.140 --> 00:59:37.710 align:middle line:84%
I have a couple colleagues,
Jal Mehta and Sarah Fine--

00:59:37.710 --> 00:59:40.230 align:middle line:84%
Jal's still at Harvard,
Sarah was there for a while

00:59:40.230 --> 00:59:42.620 align:middle line:84%
and was at High Tech High
Graduate School of Education

00:59:42.620 --> 00:59:44.387 align:middle line:84%
for a while-- who
studied schools a lot.

00:59:44.387 --> 00:59:46.970 align:middle line:84%
Studied very different schools
with very different pedagogical

00:59:46.970 --> 00:59:50.580 align:middle line:84%
philosophies and found
that, across all of them,

00:59:50.580 --> 00:59:53.630 align:middle line:84%
they tended to believe
that expertise consisted

00:59:53.630 --> 00:59:57.140 align:middle line:84%
of mastery, identity, and
creativity, that you knew

00:59:57.140 --> 01:00:00.440 align:middle line:84%
a bunch of stuff in the
domain, that you developed

01:00:00.440 --> 01:00:02.930 align:middle line:84%
an identity in that domain,
and that you were able to do

01:00:02.930 --> 01:00:05.300 align:middle line:90%
flexible, creative work.

01:00:05.300 --> 01:00:07.380 align:middle line:84%
You know a bunch of
physics equations

01:00:07.380 --> 01:00:08.400 align:middle line:90%
and when to apply them.

01:00:08.400 --> 01:00:10.890 align:middle line:84%
You feel like you're kind of
a physicist and a scientist.

01:00:10.890 --> 01:00:13.710 align:middle line:84%
And when you need to
do something novel,

01:00:13.710 --> 01:00:17.158 align:middle line:84%
like build a bridge or launch a
rocket or something like that,

01:00:17.158 --> 01:00:18.950 align:middle line:84%
you're not just following
steps, but you're

01:00:18.950 --> 01:00:22.620 align:middle line:84%
doing things that are
creative and expressive.

01:00:22.620 --> 01:00:25.520 align:middle line:90%
One way that I would--

01:00:25.520 --> 01:00:27.230 align:middle line:84%
I think the cognitive
load theorists

01:00:27.230 --> 01:00:28.940 align:middle line:84%
and situated
learning people tend

01:00:28.940 --> 01:00:32.870 align:middle line:84%
to imagine different pathways
at getting at these goals.

01:00:32.870 --> 01:00:35.330 align:middle line:84%
So the cognitive
load theorists tend

01:00:35.330 --> 01:00:38.120 align:middle line:84%
to think that mastery is
like the starting point.

01:00:38.120 --> 01:00:44.320 align:middle line:84%
If you know the stuff, if you
can do the pieces, then, A,

01:00:44.320 --> 01:00:47.660 align:middle line:84%
we often most enjoy
things that we're good at.

01:00:47.660 --> 01:00:50.090 align:middle line:90%
So get you good at doing things.

01:00:50.090 --> 01:00:52.403 align:middle line:84%
There's lots of
debates in the country

01:00:52.403 --> 01:00:54.070 align:middle line:84%
right now-- we'll
talk about them some--

01:00:54.070 --> 01:00:55.995 align:middle line:90%
about teaching kids to read.

01:00:55.995 --> 01:00:57.370 align:middle line:84%
And there are some
folks who say,

01:00:57.370 --> 01:00:59.765 align:middle line:84%
we just have to let kids
explore books and find

01:00:59.765 --> 01:01:00.890 align:middle line:90%
what they're interested in.

01:01:00.890 --> 01:01:01.480 align:middle line:84%
And there are
other folks who are

01:01:01.480 --> 01:01:03.147 align:middle line:84%
like, you know what's
fun about reading,

01:01:03.147 --> 01:01:04.910 align:middle line:90%
is actually being able to read.

01:01:04.910 --> 01:01:07.810 align:middle line:84%
So teach them a bunch of
phonics and explain to them

01:01:07.810 --> 01:01:09.560 align:middle line:84%
the basics of how
to construct words,

01:01:09.560 --> 01:01:11.630 align:middle line:84%
and that's what makes
reading enjoyable.

01:01:11.630 --> 01:01:13.960 align:middle line:84%
That's what makes people
feel like readers.

01:01:13.960 --> 01:01:16.000 align:middle line:84%
Not when they have
a beautiful library

01:01:16.000 --> 01:01:17.900 align:middle line:84%
of things to choose
from, whatever they want,

01:01:17.900 --> 01:01:20.275 align:middle line:84%
but when they can actually
make sense of what's in there.

01:01:20.275 --> 01:01:21.580 align:middle line:90%
There's some sense in that.

01:01:21.580 --> 01:01:23.720 align:middle line:84%
You develop some
kind of identity,

01:01:23.720 --> 01:01:25.600 align:middle line:84%
and then you have
the opportunity

01:01:25.600 --> 01:01:28.070 align:middle line:84%
to do creative practice
that builds on that mastery.

01:01:28.070 --> 01:01:31.720 align:middle line:84%
There are other
people who say, start

01:01:31.720 --> 01:01:33.440 align:middle line:90%
with things like creativity.

01:01:33.440 --> 01:01:37.060 align:middle line:84%
Start by getting people to
play around in the space

01:01:37.060 --> 01:01:41.440 align:middle line:84%
as much as possible so that
they're motivated, so that they

01:01:41.440 --> 01:01:44.450 align:middle line:90%
say, man, I am a this.

01:01:44.450 --> 01:01:45.970 align:middle line:90%
I'm a baseball player.

01:01:45.970 --> 01:01:49.520 align:middle line:84%
I swung on a ball on
a stick a few times.

01:01:49.520 --> 01:01:51.730 align:middle line:90%
But now I am a baseball player.

01:01:51.730 --> 01:01:55.060 align:middle line:84%
And because I'm a baseball
player in my heart,

01:01:55.060 --> 01:01:57.340 align:middle line:84%
I'll memorize all the
statistics and learn the rules

01:01:57.340 --> 01:01:58.990 align:middle line:84%
and go to practice,
and eventually

01:01:58.990 --> 01:02:01.960 align:middle line:84%
do really boring
learning kinds of things,

01:02:01.960 --> 01:02:05.120 align:middle line:84%
the mastery-oriented
discipline kinds of things,

01:02:05.120 --> 01:02:08.890 align:middle line:84%
but because I know how fun it
is to play around in this space.

01:02:08.890 --> 01:02:12.910 align:middle line:84%
What do you think at MIT
are the subjects or courses

01:02:12.910 --> 01:02:15.100 align:middle line:84%
that you would take that
would be more canonically

01:02:15.100 --> 01:02:18.070 align:middle line:90%
mastery first-oriented?

01:02:18.070 --> 01:02:19.570 align:middle line:84%
What are the subjects
you would take

01:02:19.570 --> 01:02:21.640 align:middle line:84%
that you would start
with the smallest pieces,

01:02:21.640 --> 01:02:24.098 align:middle line:84%
or by contrast, if it's easier,
what are the ones that seem

01:02:24.098 --> 01:02:25.485 align:middle line:90%
to be more creativity-first?

01:02:25.485 --> 01:02:30.790 align:middle line:90%


01:02:30.790 --> 01:02:34.200 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think more bio-heavy
classes would definitely

01:02:34.200 --> 01:02:36.600 align:middle line:84%
be mastery-first,
because if you don't

01:02:36.600 --> 01:02:40.140 align:middle line:84%
understand the basics of cells,
you're not getting anywhere.

01:02:40.140 --> 01:02:42.358 align:middle line:84%
You can't go to chapter
2 without understanding--

01:02:42.358 --> 01:02:43.150 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

01:02:43.150 --> 01:02:44.692 align:middle line:84%
So it seems to be--
so eventually you

01:02:44.692 --> 01:02:46.930 align:middle line:84%
can do some pretty rad
computational biology,

01:02:46.930 --> 01:02:48.250 align:middle line:90%
biological engineering here.

01:02:48.250 --> 01:02:51.220 align:middle line:84%
But there's not a lot in
Bio 101 where they're like,

01:02:51.220 --> 01:02:54.570 align:middle line:84%
yeah, let's have cells start
generating novel proteins

01:02:54.570 --> 01:02:55.890 align:middle line:90%
or things like that.

01:02:55.890 --> 01:02:59.350 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I would say, most
of the general requirements,

01:02:59.350 --> 01:03:02.820 align:middle line:84%
especially the chemistry
requirements and the biology

01:03:02.820 --> 01:03:04.780 align:middle line:84%
requirements, are with
the hopes that, hey,

01:03:04.780 --> 01:03:06.810 align:middle line:84%
if you learned a little
bit about this, maybe

01:03:06.810 --> 01:03:10.260 align:middle line:84%
then you become interested and
want to go further and want

01:03:10.260 --> 01:03:11.808 align:middle line:90%
that to be your major.

01:03:11.808 --> 01:03:12.600 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

01:03:12.600 --> 01:03:15.360 align:middle line:84%
So which ones do that through
giving you a bunch of facts,

01:03:15.360 --> 01:03:18.410 align:middle line:84%
and which ones do that by
letting you play the whole game?

01:03:18.410 --> 01:03:22.550 align:middle line:90%


01:03:22.550 --> 01:03:25.050 align:middle line:84%
I mean, my understanding is
that a number of the engineering

01:03:25.050 --> 01:03:27.160 align:middle line:84%
departments-- like in
mechanical engineering,

01:03:27.160 --> 01:03:29.610 align:middle line:84%
you'd be more likely,
earlier in your career,

01:03:29.610 --> 01:03:32.005 align:middle line:84%
to be like, we're
going to build a robot.

01:03:32.005 --> 01:03:33.380 align:middle line:84%
Do you know how
to build a robot?

01:03:33.380 --> 01:03:33.740 align:middle line:90%
No.

01:03:33.740 --> 01:03:35.040 align:middle line:84%
Are you going to be good
at building a robot?

01:03:35.040 --> 01:03:35.210 align:middle line:90%
No.

01:03:35.210 --> 01:03:36.570 align:middle line:84%
You're going to
build a lousy robot.

01:03:36.570 --> 01:03:37.980 align:middle line:84%
We're going to let you
build a crummy robot.

01:03:37.980 --> 01:03:40.397 align:middle line:84%
And some TA is going to put
their arm around your shoulder

01:03:40.397 --> 01:03:41.923 align:middle line:90%
and be like, that robot's OK.

01:03:41.923 --> 01:03:44.090 align:middle line:84%
That robot's only OK because
you don't know anything

01:03:44.090 --> 01:03:45.120 align:middle line:90%
about control systems.

01:03:45.120 --> 01:03:46.020 align:middle line:90%
But guess what?

01:03:46.020 --> 01:03:47.725 align:middle line:84%
Guess what one of our
junior classes is?

01:03:47.725 --> 01:03:48.600 align:middle line:90%
It's control systems.

01:03:48.600 --> 01:03:50.610 align:middle line:84%
And you're like, I can't
wait to take Control Systems.

01:03:50.610 --> 01:03:51.420 align:middle line:90%
It's going to be boring.

01:03:51.420 --> 01:03:52.640 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be a
bunch of equations

01:03:52.640 --> 01:03:54.240 align:middle line:84%
and other Psets and
things like that.

01:03:54.240 --> 01:03:56.600 align:middle line:84%
But there's a robot
that you want to drive.

01:03:56.600 --> 01:03:58.490 align:middle line:84%
The NEAT program,
I think, at MIT

01:03:58.490 --> 01:04:00.350 align:middle line:84%
is really about trying
to create a bunch

01:04:00.350 --> 01:04:01.912 align:middle line:84%
of these experiential
experiences.

01:04:01.912 --> 01:04:04.370 align:middle line:84%
I mean, historically, if you
were to look at our curriculum

01:04:04.370 --> 01:04:07.320 align:middle line:84%
across MIT, many more things
would be much more like this.

01:04:07.320 --> 01:04:10.160 align:middle line:84%
I mean, our math curriculum, I
think, is very much this way.

01:04:10.160 --> 01:04:12.650 align:middle line:84%
You're just going to do math
that other people have figured

01:04:12.650 --> 01:04:13.710 align:middle line:90%
out for years and years.

01:04:13.710 --> 01:04:16.290 align:middle line:84%
And maybe, by the time
you get to the end of MIT,

01:04:16.290 --> 01:04:18.410 align:middle line:84%
but you might have to
wait till graduate school,

01:04:18.410 --> 01:04:20.577 align:middle line:84%
you can do some new math
that someone else has never

01:04:20.577 --> 01:04:21.830 align:middle line:90%
done before.

01:04:21.830 --> 01:04:24.980 align:middle line:84%
My wife teaches in the
Materials Science department.

01:04:24.980 --> 01:04:27.752 align:middle line:84%
And some of the stuff
with the Breaker Lab

01:04:27.752 --> 01:04:29.210 align:middle line:84%
that they just
developed and things

01:04:29.210 --> 01:04:32.620 align:middle line:84%
like that, where they did
an early exercise in one

01:04:32.620 --> 01:04:34.840 align:middle line:84%
of her classes, which
is, build products

01:04:34.840 --> 01:04:37.090 align:middle line:84%
from the worst
possible materials.

01:04:37.090 --> 01:04:39.490 align:middle line:84%
So phone cases that
were made of sponges

01:04:39.490 --> 01:04:41.582 align:middle line:84%
and absorbed water,
or locks that

01:04:41.582 --> 01:04:43.540 align:middle line:84%
were made of clear plastic,
where you could see

01:04:43.540 --> 01:04:44.707 align:middle line:90%
all the tumblers and things.

01:04:44.707 --> 01:04:46.690 align:middle line:84%
Basically, play
around with materials.

01:04:46.690 --> 01:04:47.780 align:middle line:90%
Do stuff with materials.

01:04:47.780 --> 01:04:50.072 align:middle line:84%
And then you're going to take
some super boring, really

01:04:50.072 --> 01:04:52.430 align:middle line:84%
hard classes, but you're
motivated to take those things.

01:04:52.430 --> 01:04:54.880 align:middle line:84%
So I think you can look
across environments

01:04:54.880 --> 01:04:57.670 align:middle line:84%
and see how they choose
different pathways

01:04:57.670 --> 01:05:00.450 align:middle line:84%
through these different-- and if
you look at reading instruction

01:05:00.450 --> 01:05:02.950 align:middle line:84%
in the United States, there's
a huge debate right now about,

01:05:02.950 --> 01:05:05.290 align:middle line:84%
do you start with mastery
and facts and pieces

01:05:05.290 --> 01:05:08.890 align:middle line:84%
and build up to readers, or do
you start by exposing people

01:05:08.890 --> 01:05:11.410 align:middle line:84%
to books and letters and words
and the things they love.

01:05:11.410 --> 01:05:15.220 align:middle line:84%
And lots of time, in
almost no course of study

01:05:15.220 --> 01:05:17.470 align:middle line:84%
is the answer only
one of those things.

01:05:17.470 --> 01:05:19.580 align:middle line:84%
Nobody believes you
should just do creativity.

01:05:19.580 --> 01:05:21.200 align:middle line:84%
I think almost all
of our subjects

01:05:21.200 --> 01:05:24.430 align:middle line:84%
have classes which are like, OK,
time to buckle down and learn

01:05:24.430 --> 01:05:24.950 align:middle line:90%
this thing.

01:05:24.950 --> 01:05:26.470 align:middle line:84%
But we can often
think about how we

01:05:26.470 --> 01:05:29.440 align:middle line:84%
order these things differently
to produce more motivation

01:05:29.440 --> 01:05:32.740 align:middle line:84%
or develop identity or other
kinds of things like that.

01:05:32.740 --> 01:05:37.673 align:middle line:84%
But again, across different
pedagogical philosophies,

01:05:37.673 --> 01:05:40.090 align:middle line:84%
it's almost always the case
that people have a very shared

01:05:40.090 --> 01:05:42.310 align:middle line:90%
idea about what expertise is.

01:05:42.310 --> 01:05:47.200 align:middle line:84%
So I think even people who
are situated learning folks,

01:05:47.200 --> 01:05:52.630 align:middle line:84%
they don't want you to
invent midwifery anew.

01:05:52.630 --> 01:05:54.820 align:middle line:84%
They want the babies
consistently delivered

01:05:54.820 --> 01:05:57.880 align:middle line:90%
in safe, healthy ways.

01:05:57.880 --> 01:06:01.780 align:middle line:84%
So it's different beliefs
about how you get there.

01:06:01.780 --> 01:06:04.600 align:middle line:84%
There are efforts to
reconcile these things, which

01:06:04.600 --> 01:06:05.570 align:middle line:90%
I didn't make you read.

01:06:05.570 --> 01:06:07.250 align:middle line:84%
But if you wanted to
explore a little bit more,

01:06:07.250 --> 01:06:09.280 align:middle line:84%
I'll give you an
introduction to one of them

01:06:09.280 --> 01:06:10.790 align:middle line:84%
and the rabbit
hole for this week.

01:06:10.790 --> 01:06:14.210 align:middle line:84%
Ken Koedinger has a couple
of things you can look at.

01:06:14.210 --> 01:06:15.930 align:middle line:84%
But the lecture he
gives is actually

01:06:15.930 --> 01:06:17.180 align:middle line:90%
the easiest way of getting it.

01:06:17.180 --> 01:06:19.060 align:middle line:84%
There's a video in
there that's quite good.

01:06:19.060 --> 01:06:21.020 align:middle line:84%
The Knowledge-Learning
Instruction Framework.

01:06:21.020 --> 01:06:22.520 align:middle line:84%
And he says, one
useful way to think

01:06:22.520 --> 01:06:28.710 align:middle line:84%
about this is that there are
more complex and less complex

01:06:28.710 --> 01:06:30.640 align:middle line:84%
knowledge components
in the things we learn.

01:06:30.640 --> 01:06:34.150 align:middle line:84%
So there are things
like Chinese characters,

01:06:34.150 --> 01:06:38.100 align:middle line:84%
and there are things like
anthropological principles

01:06:38.100 --> 01:06:40.680 align:middle line:90%
about how society are organized.

01:06:40.680 --> 01:06:43.240 align:middle line:84%
And there are different
levels of complexity.

01:06:43.240 --> 01:06:46.320 align:middle line:84%
There's also learning
processes, and there's

01:06:46.320 --> 01:06:51.270 align:middle line:84%
things like flashcards, which
is just for memorizing stuff.

01:06:51.270 --> 01:06:54.570 align:middle line:84%
And then there's things
like talk and explaining

01:06:54.570 --> 01:06:56.940 align:middle line:84%
and sense-making
and stuff like that.

01:06:56.940 --> 01:07:00.930 align:middle line:84%
And he has this theory
that, basically, you

01:07:00.930 --> 01:07:03.600 align:middle line:84%
should be matching
instructional components

01:07:03.600 --> 01:07:07.950 align:middle line:84%
to how complex the knowledge
component-- learning processes

01:07:07.950 --> 01:07:10.650 align:middle line:84%
to how complex the
knowledge components are.

01:07:10.650 --> 01:07:11.850 align:middle line:90%
He has a nice--

01:07:11.850 --> 01:07:15.060 align:middle line:84%
this is kind of a
giant science, which

01:07:15.060 --> 01:07:17.550 align:middle line:84%
is in process, where here are
a bunch of things that people

01:07:17.550 --> 01:07:18.540 align:middle line:90%
were trying to learn--

01:07:18.540 --> 01:07:22.210 align:middle line:84%
Chinese vocab, French articles,
chem models, pressure concepts.

01:07:22.210 --> 01:07:24.940 align:middle line:84%
Here are a bunch of
instructional strategies--

01:07:24.940 --> 01:07:26.640 align:middle line:84%
optimal scheduling,
feedback, worked

01:07:26.640 --> 01:07:29.100 align:middle line:84%
examples, accountable
talk, collaboration.

01:07:29.100 --> 01:07:32.600 align:middle line:84%
And you find that these
are less complex to more

01:07:32.600 --> 01:07:34.410 align:middle line:90%
complex teaching.

01:07:34.410 --> 01:07:38.910 align:middle line:84%
This is less complex to more
complex knowledge components.

01:07:38.910 --> 01:07:42.590 align:middle line:84%
And that basically, where you
get studies with statistically

01:07:42.590 --> 01:07:44.360 align:middle line:84%
significant effects
on learning is

01:07:44.360 --> 01:07:46.553 align:middle line:84%
when you move through
the middle of this space,

01:07:46.553 --> 01:07:49.220 align:middle line:84%
when you map the right knowledge
component to the right learning

01:07:49.220 --> 01:07:49.680 align:middle line:90%
process.

01:07:49.680 --> 01:07:51.290 align:middle line:84%
I don't know that Ken
Koedinger and his colleagues

01:07:51.290 --> 01:07:53.240 align:middle line:84%
have totally figured
this out either,

01:07:53.240 --> 01:07:57.300 align:middle line:84%
but it's one way to start
reconciling some of these ideas.

01:07:57.300 --> 01:07:58.520 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:07:58.520 --> 01:08:02.060 align:middle line:84%
The most important
thing for Wednesday

01:08:02.060 --> 01:08:03.870 align:middle line:84%
is that we're meeting
at the MIT Museum.

01:08:03.870 --> 01:08:06.683 align:middle line:84%
Please try to be
at the MIT Museum.

01:08:06.683 --> 01:08:09.350 align:middle line:84%
If you can get there by 1:00 and
get checked in and put your bag

01:08:09.350 --> 01:08:11.310 align:middle line:84%
in the locker, then we
can start class at 1:05.

01:08:11.310 --> 01:08:13.518 align:middle line:84%
So make every effort to get
there as soon as you can.

01:08:13.518 --> 01:08:15.470 align:middle line:90%
There's a few pieces of reading.

01:08:15.470 --> 01:08:19.050 align:middle line:84%
The Stephen Wolfram thing
is totally worth your time,

01:08:19.050 --> 01:08:20.982 align:middle line:84%
and it will require
a little attention.

01:08:20.982 --> 01:08:22.649 align:middle line:84%
You'll have to move
your way through it.

01:08:22.649 --> 01:08:24.830 align:middle line:84%
But he does just a
beautiful job explaining

01:08:24.830 --> 01:08:27.175 align:middle line:84%
the fundamentals of
generative pre-trained models,

01:08:27.175 --> 01:08:29.550 align:middle line:84%
a little bit about neural
networks, and things like that.

01:08:29.550 --> 01:08:30.770 align:middle line:84%
And then there's a
bunch of other readings

01:08:30.770 --> 01:08:33.560 align:middle line:84%
that are in there, some of which
are about the exhibits you'll

01:08:33.560 --> 01:08:36.439 align:middle line:84%
see at the museum, and some are
some other educational things

01:08:36.439 --> 01:08:40.130 align:middle line:84%
that me and some other folks
at MIT have written about GPT.

01:08:40.130 --> 01:08:42.111 align:middle line:84%
And then be sure you
refresh yourself and you

01:08:42.111 --> 01:08:44.069 align:middle line:84%
start thinking about
working on the assignment,

01:08:44.069 --> 01:08:46.220 align:middle line:84%
because you now have two of
the crucial building blocks

01:08:46.220 --> 01:08:48.080 align:middle line:84%
that you'll need for
the assignment, which

01:08:48.080 --> 01:08:50.420 align:middle line:84%
is some sense of direct
instruction and cognitive load

01:08:50.420 --> 01:08:52.890 align:middle line:84%
theory, and some sense
of constructionism,

01:08:52.890 --> 01:08:55.682 align:middle line:84%
situated learning, progressive
learning kinds of things.

01:08:55.682 --> 01:08:57.140 align:middle line:84%
And you'll need
both of those ideas

01:08:57.140 --> 01:08:58.529 align:middle line:90%
to do assignment number one.

01:08:58.529 --> 01:09:00.170 align:middle line:84%
And we'll talk a
little bit about how

01:09:00.170 --> 01:09:02.600 align:middle line:84%
we want to do assignment
number one on Wednesday.

01:09:02.600 --> 01:09:04.200 align:middle line:90%
Stay safe in the snow.

01:09:04.200 --> 01:09:05.930 align:middle line:84%
Have a wonderful
next couple of days,

01:09:05.930 --> 01:09:08.829 align:middle line:84%
and I'll see you at the
museum on Wednesday.

01:09:08.829 --> 01:09:26.000 align:middle line:90%